{"parentUuid":"1a126c4e-48d6-4dd2-9a08-be5d7e6742b7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch98mrwV2s6gnTFzxi2bJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KTywTKFHBbBzJEDMfLLLhL","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r2/A2-reader-pages.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":318,"cache_read_input_tokens":257031,"output_tokens":80,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":318,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":80,"cache_read_input_tokens":257031,"cache_creation_input_tokens":318,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":318},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":554,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01KTywTKFHBbBzJEDMfLLLhL":{"file_path":"/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r2/A2-reader-pages.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch98mo3af7RZTgYKM4Ses","requestRef":"552b070e-45d1-4946-a5f0-3e90a5863dba","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"e48bf028-b587-48a4-8d46-cd3cbf6172fb","timestamp":"2026-11-17T20:31:03.421Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","madeBy":"model","userType":"external","entrypoint":"t3-code","cwd":"/home/exedev/email-os","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","version":"2.1.326","gitBranch":"main","slug":"cached-bubbling-meadow"}
{"parentUuid":"e48bf028-b587-48a4-8d46-cd3cbf6172fb","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c047dae4-6932-41e6-af76-9b7eb335b1be","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KTywTKFHBbBzJEDMfLLLhL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Assessment A2 — reader, editors and secondary pages\n2\t\n3\tIndependent design review of f08fd85. This is Assessment A only, as assigned; no delegation or detector synthesis. Repository read-only. Reviewed PRODUCT.md, DESIGN.md’s compact specification and relevant tokens, UX-14, ADR 0003, the matching mobile CSS, components and state code. All 19 assigned PNGs were individually opened with the image tool. Browser account tabs and yellow Preview chrome are excluded. Unsupported features listed in COMMON.md are excluded.\n4\t\n5\t## 1. Design specificity\n6\t\n7\tEmail OS has a credible product identity in its behavior: explicit read state, local-only drafting, device-local reminders, incomplete-search coverage and confirmed-versus-pending outcomes all express “focused, precise, trustworthy.” The warm charcoal, restrained lavender and absence of decorative cards support that identity. Its visual authorship is less complete on phones: a six-icon reader dock, desktop-density Contact links and a persistent operations notice feel inherited from the keyboard client. Settings and Activity are appropriately plain; they do not need theatrical branding. The next design step is precision about action scope and recovery on touch, rather than more visual personality. The reader is recognizably Email OS when it reports truth; it becomes category-interchangeable when the user must decode its unlabeled controls.\n8\t\n9\t## 2. Nielsen heuristics\n10\t\n11\tScores apply only to the assigned surfaces; 4 means genuinely excellent. **Total: 26/40.**\n12\t\n13\t| Heuristic | Score | Key issue / qualification |\n14\t|---|---:|---|\n15\t| 1. Visibility of system status | 3 | Excellent factual states, but a confirmed action becomes a persistent amber expiry notice unrelated to the current screen. |\n16\t| 2. Match with the real world | 2 | A checkmark means archive/restore, while up/down means previous/next conversation; these meanings are not visible on touch. |\n17\t| 3. User control and freedom | 3 | Undo, protected draft closure and discard confirmation are sound; settled notices have no visible dismissal. |\n18\t| 4. Consistency and standards | 2 | Contact’s 24px controls and hover-only affordances break the otherwise generous mobile control system. |\n19\t| 5. Error prevention | 3 | Draft flush protection and destructive confirmation are strong; the same check glyph represents opposing conversation actions. |\n20\t| 6. Recognition rather than recall | 2 | Reader action scope and the editable search-query heading require prior knowledge or experimentation. |\n21\t| 7. Flexibility and efficiency | 3 | Bottom reader actions and field-to-field Enter support fast use; six dock choices dilute the primary decision. |\n22\t| 8. Aesthetic and minimalist design | 3 | Strong restraint and readable prose; the expired notice competes with content and Contact lacks mobile rhythm. |\n23\t| 9. Error recognition, diagnosis and recovery | 3 | Draft failures preserve text and provide Retry; expiry recovery describes a control without taking the user to the affected conversation. |\n24\t| 10. Help and documentation | 2 | Syntax and local-draft disclosures are useful, but critical touch meanings still live in titles/hidden text. |\n25\t\n26\t## 3. Cognitive load\n27\t\n28\tThis is a checklist of observed failure points, not a claim that every screen is overloaded. The reader with an expired notice fails five items; Settings and the empty Activity page are substantially calmer.\n29\t\n30\t| Checklist item | Result | Evidence |\n31\t|---|---|---|\n32\t| Single focus | Fail in expired-notice state | Historical action, warning, Reply and action dock compete with the open message. |\n33\t| Chunking ≤4 per group | Fail in reader dock | Six unlabelled controls share one undifferentiated pill. |\n34\t| Grouping | Pass with caveat | Messages, sheet rows and two three-option swipe fieldsets have clear boundaries; dock navigation and mutations need separation. |\n35\t| Visual hierarchy | Pass with caveat | Subject → sender → body is legible; amber expiry text can outrank current content. |\n36\t| One thing at a time | Fail in expired-notice state | The user is asked to recover an older action while reading or starting a search. |\n37\t| ≤4 visible options per decision | Fail | Reader dock has six options; browser More sheet displays five rows, one disabled. |\n38\t| Working memory | Fail | Recovery names “Mark not done” for an earlier subject, but the visible check operates on the current conversation. |\n39\t| Progressive disclosure | Pass with caveat | Details, message More, search syntax, Cc/Subject and settings explanation are sensibly disclosed; some essential labels are hidden too. |\n40\t\n41\t**Decision inventories above four:** reader dock = Star, Remind, Mark done, Previous, Next, More (six), plus a separate Reply action. Browser reader More = Mark read, Reply, Files and links, Contact details, Commands (five displayed; four enabled in this capture). Android omits Files and links, leaving four. Contact displays five recent-mail choices in one group. Search results expose more than four conversation choices; that is a scalable retrieval list, not a reason to arbitrarily truncate results. The six-item Search syntax list exists behind disclosure and is reference material, not six simultaneous actions. Settings exposes six radios overall but only three options per independent direction decision; it passes. Native hour/minute selectors contain 24/60 values in code; their opened presentation was not supplied, so no visible-option claim is made for them.\n42\t\n43\t## 4. Emotional journey\n44\t\n45\t**Peak:** opening a thread gives the newest message emphasis, readable 16px/1.5 prose and an immediately reachable Reply affordance. Mark done then advances to another conversation while reporting the previous subject and Confirmed with Undo: an efficient and reassuring moment (`reader-undo-toast.png`).\n46\t\n47\t**Valley and end:** when Undo expires, the experience ends with a warning rather than completion. Both Android captures preserve the same old operation while the user has moved elsewhere. The copy is factually helpful but feels like unresolved work. It also names a reversal whose scope is the previous conversation, not the visible one.\n48\t\n49\t**Leaving a draft:** `Saved locally · never sent` is excellent reassurance in Android. The X actually invokes Save and close, but its visible shape says only “dismiss”; the only bottom action is Discard. A first-timer must trust an unlabeled exit. This is a presentation problem, not evidence of lost drafts: `useDrafts.ts:880` onward waits for the protected save and keeps the editor open on failure.\n50\t\n51\t**Failed save:** code provides a specific alert, preserved buffer, and a 48px Retry save before the fields (`DraftEditorCore.vue:285–292`; `draft-errors.ts`). That is strong recovery design. No failure screenshot was supplied; recovery geometry with the keyboard open remains unverified.\n52\t\n53\t**Changing a setting:** reminder changes save immediately, say Saved, and restore confirmed values on failure (`ReminderSettings.vue:33–52`). Swipe settings also report Saved/Could not save, but the shared status sits after both fieldsets and does not identify which direction changed. The initial screen should say “Changes save immediately”; success should say “Swipe left saved” or “Default time saved,” only after success. Opening the explanatory note is safe and useful, though it pushes later controls down.\n54\t\n55\t**Keyboard-up:** `compose-keyboard.png` shows a shortened editor with the To field and footer still visible, but no real IME. `useVisualViewportFocus.ts:5–19` scrolls the focused element into view on viewport changes; field rows are 48px, the body has a 120px minimum, and fields scroll. These are good foundations, not proof of real Android caret visibility, long-body editing, reply context, or failure-alert behavior with the IME open. The preview-memory warning is a deliberate preview state, not an Android save failure; do not count its extra line against production compose.\n56\t\n57\t## 5. Specific strengths\n58\t\n59\t1. **Truth is part of the interface.** Android compose says Saved locally and never sent; search results disclose incomplete cached coverage; reminders explain that execution depends on the app being open. These statements prevent specific false expectations without invented success claims.\n60\t2. **Reading gets a useful hierarchy.** A 22px/600 subject, compact older-message summaries, a distinct expanded message and 16px/1.5 body separate conversation identity from prose. The neutral palette and lavender action accent serve that hierarchy without decorative clutter.\n61\t3. **Secondary complexity is mostly disclosed well.** Search syntax, message details, Cc/Subject and the settings note stay out of the main path. The message sheet has three plainly named 52px actions, and Activity’s empty state explains its purpose without a fabricated event or celebratory illustration.\n62\t\n63\t## 6. Priority issues\n64\t\n65\t### P1 — A settled action becomes an undismissable recovery banner\n66\t\n67\t**What:** The old Mark done notice survives expiry and navigation. In Android Search it covers result content; in Reader it adds a large third layer above Reply and the dock. There is no visible dismiss action or direct path to its subject.\n68\t\n69\t**Why it matters:** It converts successful completion into a persistent warning. “Mark not done restores…” can send a distracted user to the current reader’s checkmark, which applies to a different conversation.\n70\t\n71\t**Fix:** Give confirmed single-action notices a dismissible presentation after their Undo window closes. Keep the authoritative result in Activity; do not erase pending, failed or unknown outcomes automatically. Add a 48px Dismiss target and a subject-bound “View action” route to the relevant Activity entry. Render settled expiry in `--text-secondary`, reserving amber for uncertainty/attention. Recovery controls must bind the notice’s conversation, never the currently open one.\n72\t\n73\t**Evidence:** `android/reader-with-expired-notice.png`, `android/search-form-with-expired-notice.png`; `ActionNotice.vue:218` (recovery wording), `ActionNotice.vue:360` (single-action template); `useActionNotice.ts` derives the slot from `lastAction` without a presentation expiry; `mobile/notices.css:2`; `mobile/shell.css` positions `.list-dock` above the bottom clearance. This is verified product behavior predating the phone work, not harness chrome.\n74\t\n75\t### P1 — Contact never received the phone interaction treatment\n76\t\n77\t**What:** Recent subjects are packed into 24px rows. Name/address copy buttons are 24×24px and opacity zero until hover/focus. The large contact name is itself an undisclosed sender-search action.\n78\t\n79\t**Why it matters:** Adjacent recent threads are easy to mistap; copy is undiscoverable on touch. Attractive large text does not compensate for small targets or invisible actions.\n80\t\n81\t**Fix:** In `mobile/pages.css`, set `.contact-recent button { min-height:48px; height:auto; padding:10px 0; white-space:normal; overflow-wrap:anywhere; }`; add quiet row rules. Set `.contact-copy { width:48px; height:48px; opacity:1; }` in the compact scope. Put a visible “Find mail from sender” control below identity instead of requiring users to infer that the name is a button. Preserve selectable address text.\n82\t\n83\t**Evidence:** `contact.png`; `ContactPane.vue:106`, `:127`, `:141`; `style.css:1070` fixes recent buttons at 24px, `style.css:2481` sets copy size and opacity. No matching compact override exists.\n84\t\n85\t### P1 — The main reader decision is an ambiguous glyph among six peers\n86\t\n87\t**What:** Star, clock, check, up, down and ellipsis share a uniform dock. The check receives lavender but no visible action name. The same check glyph switches between Mark done and Mark not done; up/down navigate conversations, not messages.\n88\t\n89\t**Why it matters:** These are different action scopes with material consequences. Accessible labels are present, but titles and hidden text do not teach a sighted phone user what will happen.\n90\t\n91\t**Fix:** Give `[data-test=\"reader-archive-toggle\"]` a visible 13px label (“Mark done” / “Mark not done”) beside its 24px glyph and a minimum 48px height. Keep Reply in its current reachable row. Reduce the main dock to the labelled primary action, Remind and More; move Star and a separately labelled “Previous conversation” / “Next conversation” group into More. Use text/state change for the opposite action, not accent alone. Do not add a routine confirmation step to every archive.\n92\t\n93\t**Evidence:** `reader.png`, `reader-undo-toast.png`, Android reader; `ReaderView.vue:944–987`; `mobile/shell.css` `.reader-actions` uses uniform 48px controls; `mobile/reader.css` accents the enabled archive toggle. The More sheet’s duplicated Reply is less consequential than the missing primary labels.\n94\t\n95\t### P2 — Long subjects are silently cut off in the reader\n96\t\n97\t**What:** `.reader-subject` wraps long strings but caps height at 4.5em with `overflow:hidden`; at line-height 1.5 this permits three lines. The heading has no expansion affordance. The More sheet title is separately forced to one ellipsized line.\n98\t\n99\t**Why it matters:** The distinguishing portion of a long subject may be its suffix. The reader should be the place where a truncated list identity becomes fully knowable.\n100\t\n101\t**Fix:** Remove `max-height` and `overflow:hidden` from the compact subject; it is already inside `.reader-scroll`. If a collapsed subject is necessary, use an explicit three-line clamp plus a 48px “Show full subject” disclosure. Allow the sheet title to wrap to two lines while keeping the reader’s full subject available.\n102\t\n103\t**Evidence:** `mobile/reader.css:9–18`; `ReaderView.vue:1033`; `mobile/sheet.css` `.mobile-action-sheet-title`. Current fixture subjects fit, so this is a code-verified edge case, not a claim that the supplied subject is visibly missing text.\n104\t\n105\t### P2 — The safest editor exit has the least explicit presentation\n106\t\n107\t**What:** On compact layouts the X is the sole save-and-close exit, while Discard is the only settled footer action. Its Save and close label is only in `aria-label`/title.\n108\t\n109\t**Why it matters:** At the moment of interruption, a first-timer cannot confidently predict whether X preserves the draft. The control is also at the far top edge for one-handed use.\n110\t\n111\t**Fix:** Replace the visible X with a text “Save and close” header action, retaining its protected handler and minimum 48px target; allow the heading to truncate before the action. Keep the factual local-save status. On failure, continue to show the existing alert and Retry save; never close optimistically or imply that a message was sent.\n112\t\n113\t**Evidence:** `compose.png`, `reply.png`, `android/compose.png`; `ComposeCard.vue:125`, `ReplyComposer.vue:135`, `DraftEditorCore.vue:370`; protected close behavior in `useDrafts.ts:880` onward. This critiques visible communication, not save correctness.\n114\t\n115\t## 7. Persona red flags\n116\t\n117\t- **Jordan, first-timer:** the reader’s check and up/down arrows hide action scope; `.search-results-query` looks like a heading despite being the edit button (`App.vue:2315`); `.contact-name.contact-search` looks like identity despite opening search; editor X hides its save contract. Jordan gets helpful factual status but insufficient instruction about what can be touched.\n118\t- **Casey, one-handed and distracted:** `.contact-recent button` and `.contact-copy` are 24px targets; top-right `.compose-sheet-close` is the only editor exit; the historical expiry banner sits directly above the current conversation’s controls. Most other reader/sheet targets are a good 48–52px—do not solve Casey’s problems by shrinking those.\n119\t- **Riley, long content/failures/interruptions:** `.reader-subject` silently clips beyond three lines; reply `.field-summary` ellipsizes the actual address in `reply.png` (tapping reveals editing, but the initial identity is incomplete); settled expiry persists across task changes. Real IME plus failure/recovery remains an evidence gap. Code provides buffer preservation and retry, so data loss is not alleged.\n120\t\n121\t## 8. Minor observations\n122\t\n123\t- `.message-details summary` has no compact 48px minimum (`style.css:2397`); the 15px text-height disclosure is a smaller target than neighboring message More. Make it an inline-flex 48px row.\n124\t- Browser reader shows a prominent white focus outline around the latest message header. Code deliberately focuses the active toggle. Its visibility may reflect the capture’s keyboard/focus modality; Android does not show it. Do not count it as a permanent touch-state border defect.\n125\t- `reader.png` and `reader-scrolled-end.png` show the same short thread. They do not demonstrate that a long conversation’s final body clears every notice state.\n126\t- Search results correctly disclose cached limitations, but query editing is visually hidden in a heading. Add a small pencil glyph or “Edit” text within the existing 48px query button; retain full query accessibility text when ellipsized.\n127\t- Settings radio selection is native blue while the main product action accent is lavender. `accent-color:var(--focus-accent)` would make selected preferences coherent without changing their familiar shape. Lower priority than touch and recovery fixes.\n128\t- Reminders and Activity use terse, useful empty states. Empty space here is acceptable; do not fill it with promotional cards or unsupported actions. “No actions yet” would use plainer vocabulary than “No operations yet.”\n129\t- The browser preview’s non-durable draft message is intentionally amber; Android’s durable save line is neutral. Treating these as one inconsistent production state would be a false finding.\n130\t\n131\t## Provocative questions\n132\t\n133\t1. If “done” is the product’s central promise, why does its most important phone control still need a tooltip to explain its verb—and reuse the same glyph for the opposite verb?\n134\t2. When an action is confirmed and Undo expires, why must the user keep carrying that action into the next task instead of finding its durable record in Activity?\n135\t3. Which is more faithful to “precise”: preserving desktop Contact density, or giving every phone action a visible name and enough space to hit it once?\n136\t"}]},"uuid":"c98b45fe-d3ae-44e5-9842-3574ae2a4086","timestamp":"2026-11-17T20:31:03.478Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r2/A2-reader-pages.md","content":"# Assessment A2 — reader, editors and secondary pages\n\nIndependent design review of f08fd85. This is Assessment A only, as assigned; no delegation or detector synthesis. Repository read-only. Reviewed PRODUCT.md, DESIGN.md’s compact specification and relevant tokens, UX-14, ADR 0003, the matching mobile CSS, components and state code. All 19 assigned PNGs were individually opened with the image tool. Browser account tabs and yellow Preview chrome are excluded. Unsupported features listed in COMMON.md are excluded.\n\n## 1. Design specificity\n\nEmail OS has a credible product identity in its behavior: explicit read state, local-only drafting, device-local reminders, incomplete-search coverage and confirmed-versus-pending outcomes all express “focused, precise, trustworthy.” The warm charcoal, restrained lavender and absence of decorative cards support that identity. Its visual authorship is less complete on phones: a six-icon reader dock, desktop-density Contact links and a persistent operations notice feel inherited from the keyboard client. Settings and Activity are appropriately plain; they do not need theatrical branding. The next design step is precision about action scope and recovery on touch, rather than more visual personality. The reader is recognizably Email OS when it reports truth; it becomes category-interchangeable when the user must decode its unlabeled controls.\n\n## 2. Nielsen heuristics\n\nScores apply only to the assigned surfaces; 4 means genuinely excellent. **Total: 26/40.**\n\n| Heuristic | Score | Key issue / qualification |\n|---|---:|---|\n| 1. Visibility of system status | 3 | Excellent factual states, but a confirmed action becomes a persistent amber expiry notice unrelated to the current screen. |\n| 2. Match with the real world | 2 | A checkmark means archive/restore, while up/down means previous/next conversation; these meanings are not visible on touch. |\n| 3. User control and freedom | 3 | Undo, protected draft closure and discard confirmation are sound; settled notices have no visible dismissal. |\n| 4. Consistency and standards | 2 | Contact’s 24px controls and hover-only affordances break the otherwise generous mobile control system. |\n| 5. Error prevention | 3 | Draft flush protection and destructive confirmation are strong; the same check glyph represents opposing conversation actions. |\n| 6. Recognition rather than recall | 2 | Reader action scope and the editable search-query heading require prior knowledge or experimentation. |\n| 7. Flexibility and efficiency | 3 | Bottom reader actions and field-to-field Enter support fast use; six dock choices dilute the primary decision. |\n| 8. Aesthetic and minimalist design | 3 | Strong restraint and readable prose; the expired notice competes with content and Contact lacks mobile rhythm. |\n| 9. Error recognition, diagnosis and recovery | 3 | Draft failures preserve text and provide Retry; expiry recovery describes a control without taking the user to the affected conversation. |\n| 10. Help and documentation | 2 | Syntax and local-draft disclosures are useful, but critical touch meanings still live in titles/hidden text. |\n\n## 3. Cognitive load\n\nThis is a checklist of observed failure points, not a claim that every screen is overloaded. The reader with an expired notice fails five items; Settings and the empty Activity page are substantially calmer.\n\n| Checklist item | Result | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Single focus | Fail in expired-notice state | Historical action, warning, Reply and action dock compete with the open message. |\n| Chunking ≤4 per group | Fail in reader dock | Six unlabelled controls share one undifferentiated pill. |\n| Grouping | Pass with caveat | Messages, sheet rows and two three-option swipe fieldsets have clear boundaries; dock navigation and mutations need separation. |\n| Visual hierarchy | Pass with caveat | Subject → sender → body is legible; amber expiry text can outrank current content. |\n| One thing at a time | Fail in expired-notice state | The user is asked to recover an older action while reading or starting a search. |\n| ≤4 visible options per decision | Fail | Reader dock has six options; browser More sheet displays five rows, one disabled. |\n| Working memory | Fail | Recovery names “Mark not done” for an earlier subject, but the visible check operates on the current conversation. |\n| Progressive disclosure | Pass with caveat | Details, message More, search syntax, Cc/Subject and settings explanation are sensibly disclosed; some essential labels are hidden too. |\n\n**Decision inventories above four:** reader dock = Star, Remind, Mark done, Previous, Next, More (six), plus a separate Reply action. Browser reader More = Mark read, Reply, Files and links, Contact details, Commands (five displayed; four enabled in this capture). Android omits Files and links, leaving four. Contact displays five recent-mail choices in one group. Search results expose more than four conversation choices; that is a scalable retrieval list, not a reason to arbitrarily truncate results. The six-item Search syntax list exists behind disclosure and is reference material, not six simultaneous actions. Settings exposes six radios overall but only three options per independent direction decision; it passes. Native hour/minute selectors contain 24/60 values in code; their opened presentation was not supplied, so no visible-option claim is made for them.\n\n## 4. Emotional journey\n\n**Peak:** opening a thread gives the newest message emphasis, readable 16px/1.5 prose and an immediately reachable Reply affordance. Mark done then advances to another conversation while reporting the previous subject and Confirmed with Undo: an efficient and reassuring moment (`reader-undo-toast.png`).\n\n**Valley and end:** when Undo expires, the experience ends with a warning rather than completion. Both Android captures preserve the same old operation while the user has moved elsewhere. The copy is factually helpful but feels like unresolved work. It also names a reversal whose scope is the previous conversation, not the visible one.\n\n**Leaving a draft:** `Saved locally · never sent` is excellent reassurance in Android. The X actually invokes Save and close, but its visible shape says only “dismiss”; the only bottom action is Discard. A first-timer must trust an unlabeled exit. This is a presentation problem, not evidence of lost drafts: `useDrafts.ts:880` onward waits for the protected save and keeps the editor open on failure.\n\n**Failed save:** code provides a specific alert, preserved buffer, and a 48px Retry save before the fields (`DraftEditorCore.vue:285–292`; `draft-errors.ts`). That is strong recovery design. No failure screenshot was supplied; recovery geometry with the keyboard open remains unverified.\n\n**Changing a setting:** reminder changes save immediately, say Saved, and restore confirmed values on failure (`ReminderSettings.vue:33–52`). Swipe settings also report Saved/Could not save, but the shared status sits after both fieldsets and does not identify which direction changed. The initial screen should say “Changes save immediately”; success should say “Swipe left saved” or “Default time saved,” only after success. Opening the explanatory note is safe and useful, though it pushes later controls down.\n\n**Keyboard-up:** `compose-keyboard.png` shows a shortened editor with the To field and footer still visible, but no real IME. `useVisualViewportFocus.ts:5–19` scrolls the focused element into view on viewport changes; field rows are 48px, the body has a 120px minimum, and fields scroll. These are good foundations, not proof of real Android caret visibility, long-body editing, reply context, or failure-alert behavior with the IME open. The preview-memory warning is a deliberate preview state, not an Android save failure; do not count its extra line against production compose.\n\n## 5. Specific strengths\n\n1. **Truth is part of the interface.** Android compose says Saved locally and never sent; search results disclose incomplete cached coverage; reminders explain that execution depends on the app being open. These statements prevent specific false expectations without invented success claims.\n2. **Reading gets a useful hierarchy.** A 22px/600 subject, compact older-message summaries, a distinct expanded message and 16px/1.5 body separate conversation identity from prose. The neutral palette and lavender action accent serve that hierarchy without decorative clutter.\n3. **Secondary complexity is mostly disclosed well.** Search syntax, message details, Cc/Subject and the settings note stay out of the main path. The message sheet has three plainly named 52px actions, and Activity’s empty state explains its purpose without a fabricated event or celebratory illustration.\n\n## 6. Priority issues\n\n### P1 — A settled action becomes an undismissable recovery banner\n\n**What:** The old Mark done notice survives expiry and navigation. In Android Search it covers result content; in Reader it adds a large third layer above Reply and the dock. There is no visible dismiss action or direct path to its subject.\n\n**Why it matters:** It converts successful completion into a persistent warning. “Mark not done restores…” can send a distracted user to the current reader’s checkmark, which applies to a different conversation.\n\n**Fix:** Give confirmed single-action notices a dismissible presentation after their Undo window closes. Keep the authoritative result in Activity; do not erase pending, failed or unknown outcomes automatically. Add a 48px Dismiss target and a subject-bound “View action” route to the relevant Activity entry. Render settled expiry in `--text-secondary`, reserving amber for uncertainty/attention. Recovery controls must bind the notice’s conversation, never the currently open one.\n\n**Evidence:** `android/reader-with-expired-notice.png`, `android/search-form-with-expired-notice.png`; `ActionNotice.vue:218` (recovery wording), `ActionNotice.vue:360` (single-action template); `useActionNotice.ts` derives the slot from `lastAction` without a presentation expiry; `mobile/notices.css:2`; `mobile/shell.css` positions `.list-dock` above the bottom clearance. This is verified product behavior predating the phone work, not harness chrome.\n\n### P1 — Contact never received the phone interaction treatment\n\n**What:** Recent subjects are packed into 24px rows. Name/address copy buttons are 24×24px and opacity zero until hover/focus. The large contact name is itself an undisclosed sender-search action.\n\n**Why it matters:** Adjacent recent threads are easy to mistap; copy is undiscoverable on touch. Attractive large text does not compensate for small targets or invisible actions.\n\n**Fix:** In `mobile/pages.css`, set `.contact-recent button { min-height:48px; height:auto; padding:10px 0; white-space:normal; overflow-wrap:anywhere; }`; add quiet row rules. Set `.contact-copy { width:48px; height:48px; opacity:1; }` in the compact scope. Put a visible “Find mail from sender” control below identity instead of requiring users to infer that the name is a button. Preserve selectable address text.\n\n**Evidence:** `contact.png`; `ContactPane.vue:106`, `:127`, `:141`; `style.css:1070` fixes recent buttons at 24px, `style.css:2481` sets copy size and opacity. No matching compact override exists.\n\n### P1 — The main reader decision is an ambiguous glyph among six peers\n\n**What:** Star, clock, check, up, down and ellipsis share a uniform dock. The check receives lavender but no visible action name. The same check glyph switches between Mark done and Mark not done; up/down navigate conversations, not messages.\n\n**Why it matters:** These are different action scopes with material consequences. Accessible labels are present, but titles and hidden text do not teach a sighted phone user what will happen.\n\n**Fix:** Give `[data-test=\"reader-archive-toggle\"]` a visible 13px label (“Mark done” / “Mark not done”) beside its 24px glyph and a minimum 48px height. Keep Reply in its current reachable row. Reduce the main dock to the labelled primary action, Remind and More; move Star and a separately labelled “Previous conversation” / “Next conversation” group into More. Use text/state change for the opposite action, not accent alone. Do not add a routine confirmation step to every archive.\n\n**Evidence:** `reader.png`, `reader-undo-toast.png`, Android reader; `ReaderView.vue:944–987`; `mobile/shell.css` `.reader-actions` uses uniform 48px controls; `mobile/reader.css` accents the enabled archive toggle. The More sheet’s duplicated Reply is less consequential than the missing primary labels.\n\n### P2 — Long subjects are silently cut off in the reader\n\n**What:** `.reader-subject` wraps long strings but caps height at 4.5em with `overflow:hidden`; at line-height 1.5 this permits three lines. The heading has no expansion affordance. The More sheet title is separately forced to one ellipsized line.\n\n**Why it matters:** The distinguishing portion of a long subject may be its suffix. The reader should be the place where a truncated list identity becomes fully knowable.\n\n**Fix:** Remove `max-height` and `overflow:hidden` from the compact subject; it is already inside `.reader-scroll`. If a collapsed subject is necessary, use an explicit three-line clamp plus a 48px “Show full subject” disclosure. Allow the sheet title to wrap to two lines while keeping the reader’s full subject available.\n\n**Evidence:** `mobile/reader.css:9–18`; `ReaderView.vue:1033`; `mobile/sheet.css` `.mobile-action-sheet-title`. Current fixture subjects fit, so this is a code-verified edge case, not a claim that the supplied subject is visibly missing text.\n\n### P2 — The safest editor exit has the least explicit presentation\n\n**What:** On compact layouts the X is the sole save-and-close exit, while Discard is the only settled footer action. Its Save and close label is only in `aria-label`/title.\n\n**Why it matters:** At the moment of interruption, a first-timer cannot confidently predict whether X preserves the draft. The control is also at the far top edge for one-handed use.\n\n**Fix:** Replace the visible X with a text “Save and close” header action, retaining its protected handler and minimum 48px target; allow the heading to truncate before the action. Keep the factual local-save status. On failure, continue to show the existing alert and Retry save; never close optimistically or imply that a message was sent.\n\n**Evidence:** `compose.png`, `reply.png`, `android/compose.png`; `ComposeCard.vue:125`, `ReplyComposer.vue:135`, `DraftEditorCore.vue:370`; protected close behavior in `useDrafts.ts:880` onward. This critiques visible communication, not save correctness.\n\n## 7. Persona red flags\n\n- **Jordan, first-timer:** the reader’s check and up/down arrows hide action scope; `.search-results-query` looks like a heading despite being the edit button (`App.vue:2315`); `.contact-name.contact-search` looks like identity despite opening search; editor X hides its save contract. Jordan gets helpful factual status but insufficient instruction about what can be touched.\n- **Casey, one-handed and distracted:** `.contact-recent button` and `.contact-copy` are 24px targets; top-right `.compose-sheet-close` is the only editor exit; the historical expiry banner sits directly above the current conversation’s controls. Most other reader/sheet targets are a good 48–52px—do not solve Casey’s problems by shrinking those.\n- **Riley, long content/failures/interruptions:** `.reader-subject` silently clips beyond three lines; reply `.field-summary` ellipsizes the actual address in `reply.png` (tapping reveals editing, but the initial identity is incomplete); settled expiry persists across task changes. Real IME plus failure/recovery remains an evidence gap. Code provides buffer preservation and retry, so data loss is not alleged.\n\n## 8. Minor observations\n\n- `.message-details summary` has no compact 48px minimum (`style.css:2397`); the 15px text-height disclosure is a smaller target than neighboring message More. Make it an inline-flex 48px row.\n- Browser reader shows a prominent white focus outline around the latest message header. Code deliberately focuses the active toggle. Its visibility may reflect the capture’s keyboard/focus modality; Android does not show it. Do not count it as a permanent touch-state border defect.\n- `reader.png` and `reader-scrolled-end.png` show the same short thread. They do not demonstrate that a long conversation’s final body clears every notice state.\n- Search results correctly disclose cached limitations, but query editing is visually hidden in a heading. Add a small pencil glyph or “Edit” text within the existing 48px query button; retain full query accessibility text when ellipsized.\n- Settings radio selection is native blue while the main product action accent is lavender. `accent-color:var(--focus-accent)` would make selected preferences coherent without changing their familiar shape. Lower priority than touch and recovery fixes.\n- Reminders and Activity use terse, useful empty states. Empty space here is acceptable; do not fill it with promotional cards or unsupported actions. “No actions yet” would use plainer vocabulary than “No operations yet.”\n- The browser preview’s non-durable draft message is intentionally amber; Android’s durable save line is neutral. Treating these as one inconsistent production state would be a false finding.\n\n## Provocative questions\n\n1. If “done” is the product’s central promise, why does its most important phone control still need a tooltip to explain its verb—and reuse the same glyph for the opposite verb?\n2. When an action is confirmed and Undo expires, why must the user keep carrying that action into the next task instead of finding its durable record in Activity?\n3. Which is more faithful to “precise”: preserving desktop Contact density, or giving every phone action a visible name and enough space to hit it once?\n","numLines":136,"startLine":1,"totalLines":136}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e48bf028-b587-48a4-8d46-cd3cbf6172fb","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"t3-code","cwd":"/home/exedev/email-os","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","version":"2.1.326","gitBranch":"main","slug":"cached-bubbling-meadow"}
{"type":"api-request","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","id":"412e4b9e-f1c8-4e25-8d75-4e34edb74129","timestamp":"2026-11-17T20:31:03.531Z","version":"2.1.326","querySource":"sdk","shapeHash":"910f92d9bd9f283f9ce514220272f6b9ed1f306559080f25290301d617c09838","params":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","betas":["claude-code-20250219","oauth-2025-04-20","interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14","context-management-2025-06-27","prompt-caching-scope-2026-01-05","per-turn-control-2026-07-01","mid-conversation-tool-changes-2026-07-01","inline-tools-2026-09-15","advisor-tool-2026-03-01","thinking-token-count-2026-05-13","advanced-tool-use-2025-11-20","mid-conversation-system-clear-at-2026-08-21","effort-2025-11-24","thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01","extended-cache-ttl-2025-04-11","cache-diagnosis-2026-04-07"],"max_tokens":128000,"thinking":{"type":"adaptive","display":"summarized"},"context_management":{"edits":[{"type":"clear_thinking_20251015","keep":"all"}]},"output_config":{"effort":"high"}},"base":"552b070e-45d1-4946-a5f0-3e90a5863dba","keep":340,"tail":["5f28e308719ac43793ada7ea8925158a599ff553cccad83638de56455c8d2251","2779d45250c5bde003ff8611e40e5d58d78706fd3a64d8bd1c8198d153c75e43"],"cacheControl":[{"path":["system",1],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h","scope":"global"}},{"path":["system",2],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}},{"path":["messages",340,"content",0],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}},{"path":["messages",341,"content",0],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}}],"requestDigest":"0bd5714e415aefecac7568af9a5662ec300511d8d83bf3af9f5da9a1f14b6c65"}
