{"parentUuid":"9adc2bf1-5bcb-4fad-b0ed-96beba79385d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch98ngu1YhJLoZPd3C9qU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HDMYFCS5ksWWbqyfPCa9ry","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r2/A1-list.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":479,"cache_read_input_tokens":263887,"output_tokens":76,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":479,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":76,"cache_read_input_tokens":263887,"cache_creation_input_tokens":479,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":479},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":537,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01HDMYFCS5ksWWbqyfPCa9ry":{"file_path":"/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r2/A1-list.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch98nbtPiMj1UBY3wsbrb","requestRef":"6be49d6c-d8dd-4a30-915c-85983c28dacf","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"a1c3754f-34fb-49f9-b0c0-644c8e3fd2b1","timestamp":"2026-11-17T20:31:14.453Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","madeBy":"model","userType":"external","entrypoint":"t3-code","cwd":"/home/exedev/email-os","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","version":"2.1.326","gitBranch":"main","slug":"cached-bubbling-meadow"}
{"parentUuid":"a1c3754f-34fb-49f9-b0c0-644c8e3fd2b1","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9f6f758c-81e7-4f81-8936-fdb94b5d7a51","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HDMYFCS5ksWWbqyfPCa9ry","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Assessment A1 — mail list surfaces\n2\t\n3\tIndependent design review of f08fd85. Mode: Operate. I used the supplied brief in place of an interview, inspected all 14 assigned PNGs with the image tool, and verified findings against source. No delegation, detector, live browser, or repository edits. This is Assessment A, not a synthesis with Assessment B.\n4\t\n5\tInspected: browser/inbox.png, inbox-scrolled-end.png, drawer.png, swipe-done.png, swipe-done-armed.png, swipe-remind-armed.png, selection.png, selection-with-notice.png, inbox-undo-toast.png, reminder-picker.png, palette.png, drafts.png; android/inbox.png and android/selection.png. Browser account tabs and the yellow Preview label are harness chrome and excluded. Literal markup and repetitive fixture subjects are test content, not copy defects. The assigned Android images do not show expired Undo; expiry findings below are code-based and the supplied brief identifies that behavior as predating the phone work.\n6\t\n7\t## 1. Design specificity\n8\t\n9\tThis is recognizably a focused mail-processing instrument: the warm charcoal plane, restrained lavender, explicit read/unread weight, factual outcomes, and local-draft disclosure support “focused, precise, trustworthy.” It does not need decorative branding. Its strongest authorship is behavioral—distinguishing acceptance from confirmation—rather than its familiar mail-row anatomy. The phone composition is less resolved: an almost vacant Inbox header, a full-width pill containing one Inbox tab, and another full-width pill containing two icons spend considerable space on framing. Selection adds a third visual mass and outcomes a fourth. The result feels like a carefully styled desktop action model fitted into a phone shell, rather than a phone triage loop whose every square centimetre has earned its place. Keep the palette and sober voice; make scope, recovery, and action hierarchy equally precise.\n10\t\n11\t## 2. Nielsen heuristics\n12\t\n13\tScores apply only to the assigned surfaces; 4 means excellent. **Total: 25/40.**\n14\t\n15\t| Heuristic | Score | Key issue |\n16\t|---|---:|---|\n17\t| Visibility of system status | 3 | Exact outcome states are strong; Undo gives no advance indication of its deadline. |\n18\t| Match with the real world | 3 | Sender → subject → preview is natural; “Provider mail,” “Activity,” and command vocabulary demand product knowledge. |\n19\t| User control and freedom | 2 | Clear selection and Undo exist, but individual selection entry is hidden and the palette lacks a visible close control. |\n20\t| Consistency and standards | 3 | Stable glyphs, tonal hierarchy and 48px controls mostly hold; the bulk primary action alone drops to 40px. |\n21\t| Error prevention | 2 | “Mark N done” states quantity, but loaded-only scope is hidden and a quick swipe can commit without the armed cue. |\n22\t| Recognition rather than recall | 2 | Long-press selection, configurable swipe meanings, and recovery through another destination rely on remembered behavior. |\n23\t| Flexibility and efficiency | 3 | Swipe, long-press, explicit bulk setters and a searchable command list support fast processing; the picker is verbose. |\n24\t| Aesthetic and minimalist design | 3 | Rows are quiet and legible; repeated floating containers and long reminder dates compete with the mail. |\n25\t| Recognize, diagnose, recover from errors | 2 | Failure/unknown/partial outcomes are honest; “see Activity” and expired-Undo text do not themselves take the user to recovery. |\n26\t| Help and documentation | 2 | Local-only and timezone explanations help; the primary phone selection gesture has no visible or accessible local teaching cue. |\n27\t\n28\t## 3. Cognitive load — eight checks\n29\t\n30\t| Check | Result | Evidence |\n31\t|---|---|---|\n32\t| Single focus | Partial failure | Inbox focuses on mail; selection-with-notice.png asks the user to reconcile a previous action, current selection, and persistent navigation. |\n33\t| Chunking ≤4 per group | Fail | Drawer has nine destination/utility entries in one visual run; palette’s Conversation group has eight actions. |\n34\t| Grouping | Partial failure | Drawer divider is inserted by numeric index, so available capabilities determine its semantic grouping. In drawer.png it does not separate mail from utilities. |\n35\t| Visual hierarchy | Partial failure | Rows pass through weight and tone; reminder options give secondary full dates nearly the same visual authority as their labels. |\n36\t| One thing at a time | Fail | Reminder sheet exposes time entry, four suggestions, return condition and scheduling together, after several lines of setup text. |\n37\t| ≤4 visible options per decision | Fail | Drawer: nine entries. Palette: eight Conversation actions plus Select all loaded visible. Selection: four task actions plus Clear; navigation adds two more exits. Reminder time: four presets plus custom entry = five paths. |\n38\t| Working memory | Fail | Users must remember how to enter selection, that Select all means loaded rows only, and that Undo has an unseen deadline. |\n39\t| Progressive disclosure | Mixed | Reminder subject details and command search are useful disclosure; blank-query palette exposes a large command catalogue, and the picker repeats timezone detail instead of disclosing it once. |\n40\t\n41\tThese are decision-load flags, not a claim that every list must have four rows. Mail rows are content to scan, not nine unrelated commands. The drawer and blank-query palette need semantic groups; they do not need arbitrary pagination into groups of four.\n42\t\n43\t## 4. Emotional journey\n44\t\n45\t**Arrival:** calm and competent. Sender names and unread weight make the first scan straightforward. Android confirms this is not merely a browser rendering success. The conspicuously empty header and two lower pills make the product feel more ceremonial than fast, but do not block triage.\n46\t\n47\t**Peak:** the armed done swipe is the most decisive moment. Lavender fill, dark check, and stronger label make commitment tangible. However, the velocity shortcut can bypass that reassurance; the visual promise and actual trigger need to agree.\n48\t\n49\t**Valley:** selection shifts every text column 48px right and compresses subjects while a large action panel arrives. A previous-action toast above that panel makes “what I selected” and “what I just did” adjacent competing facts. The user should never have to audit the interface before auditing their selection.\n50\t\n51\t**Bulk mark done:** quantity in “Mark N done” is valuable reassurance. It is not a preview of which loaded conversations are affected, and the loaded-only qualification is hidden from sight. The handler immediately submits the group. For multiple conversations, show a lightweight scope review before submission, retaining the current exact outcome ledger afterward.\n52\t\n53\t**Undo expiry / ending:** confirmation is reassuring; a disappearing Undo followed by a persistent “Undo expired” instruction makes a successful action end on lost capability. Preserve pending/failed/unknown states, but let a settled success finish quietly and offer a direct recovery destination when the user asks for details. Do not replace truthful status with optimistic “Done.” Expiry persistence is inherited behavior, not a newly introduced mobile defect.\n54\t\n55\t## 5. Specific strengths\n56\t\n57\t- **The row anatomy works.** Sender at 16px, subject at 15px, preview at 14px, and 13px date create a restrained scan ladder; unread uses both weight and a dot. The Android image demonstrates fallback email senders and long subjects without horizontal page spill. Evidence: inbox.png, android/inbox.png; [list.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:157).\n58\t- **Commit states have material difference.** Done becomes solid lavender with dark text; Remind stays neutral. The source supplies short 120–160ms transitions and reduced-motion removal rather than gratuitous animation. Evidence: three swipe captures; [list.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:119), [useSwipeAction.ts](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:113). Timing is code-reviewed, not observed in motion.\n59\t- **Truth is part of the interface.** “Accepted locally, not yet confirmed,” confirmed/failed/unknown distinctions, group outcome counts, and “Local to this device · never sent” give this product a credible voice. Evidence: inbox-undo-toast.png, drafts.png; [ActionNotice.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:193), [DraftsList.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/DraftsList.vue:295).\n60\t\n61\t## 6. Priority issues\n62\t\n63\t### P1 — Selection hides its entry and its scope\n64\t\n65\t**What:** Before selecting, `.row-select` is `display:none`; only selection mode reveals it. Long-press accepts touch/pen and waits 500ms. There is no local single-row selection button exposed to touch exploration. The palette’s Select all loaded is a global workaround, not an equivalent individual-row entry. Once active, the bar says “Select all (18)” and hides “18 loaded.” Its primary action is only 40px high. For bulk archive, the handler submits immediately rather than presenting a scope review.\n66\t\n67\t**Why it matters:** Sam loses the direct accessible checkbox route; Casey must discover an invisible gesture; Alex can mistake a loaded subset for the mailbox. Count alone does not satisfy the product’s “preview before power” principle.\n68\t\n69\t**Fix:** Add a visible 48×48px “Select conversations” control to the list context, entering selection without selecting everything; reveal existing labelled checkboxes in that mode. Change compact scope to “1 of 18 loaded selected” and the secondary action to “Select 18 loaded.” Set `.selection-row-primary > button:not([data-test=\"selection-clear\"])` to `min-height:48px`. For count >1, intercept archive with a sheet stating “Mark 18 loaded conversations done?” and “Unloaded conversations stay unchanged,” with reviewable sender/subject identities and a “Mark 18 done” confirmation. Keep the single-item path fast.\n70\t\n71\t**Evidence:** selection.png, android/selection.png; [list.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:248), [SelectionBar.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/SelectionBar.vue:68), [useLongPressSelection.ts](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useLongPressSelection.ts:17), [App.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/App.vue:205).\n72\t\n73\t### P1 — The bottom stack consumes reading space without reserving its full height\n74\t\n75\t**What:** Selection plus a notice builds a substantial floating stack above navigation. `.list-dock` is absolute; scrollers clear only `--mobile-bottom-clearance`, which includes navigation and an optional context pill, not the dock’s variable height. Thus an end-of-list row can stop underneath the selection/notice stack. That last-row consequence is inferred from the CSS; there is no supplied selection-at-end capture. The ordinary inbox-scrolled-end.png does clear the final row and should not be reported as broken. Notices also keep expiry instructions in the same floating surface, without an in-notice recovery link or rendered dismiss control.\n76\t\n77\t**Why it matters:** The controls cover the information needed to use them confidently. Long subjects and inherited expiry copy make that coverage worse. A settled success should not become permanent visual debt.\n78\t\n79\t**Fix:** Put the dock in the compact flex layout above navigation so the list viewport shrinks by its actual height, or measure `.list-dock` with ResizeObserver and add `--mobile-dock-height` plus an 8px gap to both scroll end padding and scroll padding. Keep the selected row in the resulting visible area. Retain truthful pending/problem states. For settled notices, add Dismiss, show Undo with its remaining eligibility, and after expiry collapse to a short outcome plus a direct “View in Done” / “View Activity” action appropriate to single/group recovery. Do not automatically dismiss unresolved acceptance.\n80\t\n81\t**Evidence:** selection-with-notice.png, inbox-undo-toast.png; [shell.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/shell.css:204), [tokens.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/tokens.css:22), [notices.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/notices.css:2), [ActionNotice.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:248).\n82\t\n83\t### P1 — Swipe commitment and the armed cue use different rules\n84\t\n85\t**What:** Armed appearance begins at 35% of row width. Release also commits at just 64px if velocity exceeds 0.5px/ms. On a 412px row, that permits commitment far below the approximately 144px visual threshold. In swipe-done.png the narrow reveal clips “Mark done”; its trailing alignment and overflow clipping are real CSS behavior, not harness chrome. The glyph is selected by direction rather than configured action, so swapping preferences can pair the wrong icon with the correct label.\n86\t\n87\t**Why it matters:** A fast gesture can act before the interface has visibly promised to act. Wrong glyphs teach the wrong gesture when preferences change.\n88\t\n89\t**Fix:** Use one commit predicate for both `armed` and release; safest here is the documented 35% distance rule, removing the hidden velocity exception. Derive the glyph from the resolved action: reminder → clock; done → archive/restore according to context. In `.row-swipe-underlay`, reveal a 24px action glyph first and show the full label only when at least 128px is exposed; keep its leading edge intact. Keep the neutral/armed color difference and reduced-motion behavior.\n90\t\n91\t**Evidence:** swipe-done.png, swipe-done-armed.png, swipe-remind-armed.png; [useSwipeAction.ts](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:87), [InboxList.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:736), [list.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:90).\n92\t\n93\t### P2 — Reminder scheduling spends its hierarchy on explanation\n94\t\n95\t**What:** The sheet repeats timezone information in the introduction and each verbose timestamp. Dates wrap over three lines, while explanatory text, conversation identity, option labels, condition and primary action share nearly one type size. The sheet dominates the screen before a keyboard appears. No supplied keyboard-open picker capture establishes actual keyboard obstruction, so that is an unverified risk, not a finding.\n96\t\n97\t**Why it matters:** “Tomorrow morning” becomes a reading task. The action feels slower and more consequential than its underlying choice.\n98\t\n99\t**Fix:** In compact mode, render preset rows as a two-line grid: label at 15px/600, secondary date at 13px/400, start-aligned, `min-height:56px; gap:4px`. Use “Wed 18 Nov · 8:00 AM”; state the device timezone once as “Times in UTC” with an expandable full zone/date explanation. Preserve the exact instant in `<time datetime>` and an accessible full-date label. Keep sender and one truncated subject above time entry, with the existing disclosure for the full subject. Put condition under a closed “Return if no reply” disclosure; retain an explicit 48px Schedule reminder action after time selection.\n100\t\n101\t**Evidence:** reminder-picker.png; [ReminderPicker.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:138), [ReminderPicker.vue formatting](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:158), [pages.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/pages.css:204).\n102\t\n103\t### P2 — Drawer and palette flatten different kinds of decisions\n104\t\n105\t**What:** The drawer mixes mail views and utilities in one run. Its separator uses `index === 8`, so capability filtering changes what it separates. The blank-query palette lists eight Conversation actions, including both Star/Unstar and read/unread setters. It has no visible close button; escape/backdrop/Android Back are exit routes, but the keyboard footer is hidden on compact.\n106\t\n107\t**Why it matters:** Navigation becomes a search problem, and Commands feels like a desktop catalogue. A distracted user opening it accidentally lacks an obvious local exit.\n108\t\n109\t**Fix:** Group drawer entries by stable IDs, not rendered indices: Mail (Inbox/Starred/Drafts/Done), Follow-up (Reminders and the existing saved split when available), Utilities (Provider mail/Activity/Account/Settings), then Commands. Use 13px section captions and 8px group gaps, retaining 48px rows. In the compact palette, show four relevant shortcuts for an empty query plus “All commands”; search must still return all matching commands. Preserve explicit read/unread setters. Add a 48×48px Close button beside the query without shrinking the input below usable width.\n110\t\n111\t**Evidence:** drawer.png, palette.png; [MobileDrawer.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/mobile/MobileDrawer.vue:102), [ActionOverlay.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionOverlay.vue:90), [pages.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/pages.css:577).\n112\t\n113\tNo P0 is substantiated by these static captures and source inspection.\n114\t\n115\t## 7. Persona red flags\n116\t\n117\t- **Alex — impatient power user:** `.picker small` turns preset dates into paragraphs; `.compact-command-overlay` exposes a large blank-query inventory; the hidden velocity rule makes learned swipe feedback unreliable. “Select all (N)” conceals the loaded-scope distinction Alex is most likely to exploit at speed.\n118\t- **Casey — one-handed, distracted:** `mobile-menu` and `mobile-compose` sit at opposite top corners; most destinations require the upper-left menu. The two bottom icons are reachable, but their visible labels are absent. `.selection-row-primary` gives the consequential action a smaller target than Clear; the notice/selection stack obscures nearby mail. A 500ms long-press offers no visible entry cue.\n119\t- **Sam — screen reader / low vision:** `.row-select { display:none }` removes individual selection controls until a pointer gesture or global-selection workaround starts the mode. The 48px checkbox targets and accessible labels are good once exposed. `.selection-row-secondary` is nowrap, and its three labels plus padding have no large-text reflow rule: overflow at enlarged text is a code-based risk, not something these default-size images prove. The palette’s single combobox tab stop and lack of a close button need real TalkBack testing; source semantics alone do not establish successful operation.\n120\t\n121\t## 8. Minor observations\n122\t\n123\t- The paired top-button rings and stacked bottom pills are the most category-interchangeable part of the design. Reduce framing before adding brand flourishes. On Inbox, the lone context tab should earn its own 44px container only when another supported split is present.\n124\t- The bottom Mail icon repeats the Inbox context above it; in Drafts neither bottom icon represents the current destination. The visible Drafts heading prevents total loss of orientation, but the navigation model is semantically uneven. Evidence: drafts.png; MobileBottomBar’s `inboxOrSaved` current-state gate.\n125\t- Drafts has both the header pencil and a full-width New draft button. The empty state is usefully factual, but one reachable primary creation control would be cleaner. Populated draft rows and discard recovery were code-read, not visually validated: drafts.png is empty.\n126\t- Selection shifts text from a 20px to a 68px start inset, reducing scan continuity. Keep checkbox entry explicit and test retaining a stable text gutter during mode changes.\n127\t- Dates and previews are readable in the supplied captures; do not invent contrast failures from the muted palette. No formal contrast or large-text measurement was performed in Assessment A.\n128\t- The lavender outline on the drawer’s current Inbox row matches its programmatically assigned focus. Do not count the screenshot’s focus treatment as a second persistent selection border.\n129\t- CSS supplies a drawer transform transition, but the drawer mounts already at `translateX(0)` under `v-if`. An entrance slide is not established by that declaration alone; inspect actual motion before claiming it exists. Row and sheet timings likewise need device observation for perceived quality.\n130\t\n131\t## Provocative questions\n132\t\n133\t1. If this is a precision instrument, why is the consequential scope word—“loaded”—the part removed from sight on a phone?\n134\t2. Should an otherwise successful triage session end with a persistent explanation of the recovery window the user just lost?\n135\t3. If the phone’s main job is scan → decide → act, what do the two permanent bottom containers contribute on an Inbox with only one split and two navigation icons?\n136\t"}]},"uuid":"8ac07aba-a8bd-4b12-a87e-46d3928f7370","timestamp":"2026-11-17T20:31:14.482Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r2/A1-list.md","content":"# Assessment A1 — mail list surfaces\n\nIndependent design review of f08fd85. Mode: Operate. I used the supplied brief in place of an interview, inspected all 14 assigned PNGs with the image tool, and verified findings against source. No delegation, detector, live browser, or repository edits. This is Assessment A, not a synthesis with Assessment B.\n\nInspected: browser/inbox.png, inbox-scrolled-end.png, drawer.png, swipe-done.png, swipe-done-armed.png, swipe-remind-armed.png, selection.png, selection-with-notice.png, inbox-undo-toast.png, reminder-picker.png, palette.png, drafts.png; android/inbox.png and android/selection.png. Browser account tabs and the yellow Preview label are harness chrome and excluded. Literal markup and repetitive fixture subjects are test content, not copy defects. The assigned Android images do not show expired Undo; expiry findings below are code-based and the supplied brief identifies that behavior as predating the phone work.\n\n## 1. Design specificity\n\nThis is recognizably a focused mail-processing instrument: the warm charcoal plane, restrained lavender, explicit read/unread weight, factual outcomes, and local-draft disclosure support “focused, precise, trustworthy.” It does not need decorative branding. Its strongest authorship is behavioral—distinguishing acceptance from confirmation—rather than its familiar mail-row anatomy. The phone composition is less resolved: an almost vacant Inbox header, a full-width pill containing one Inbox tab, and another full-width pill containing two icons spend considerable space on framing. Selection adds a third visual mass and outcomes a fourth. The result feels like a carefully styled desktop action model fitted into a phone shell, rather than a phone triage loop whose every square centimetre has earned its place. Keep the palette and sober voice; make scope, recovery, and action hierarchy equally precise.\n\n## 2. Nielsen heuristics\n\nScores apply only to the assigned surfaces; 4 means excellent. **Total: 25/40.**\n\n| Heuristic | Score | Key issue |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Visibility of system status | 3 | Exact outcome states are strong; Undo gives no advance indication of its deadline. |\n| Match with the real world | 3 | Sender → subject → preview is natural; “Provider mail,” “Activity,” and command vocabulary demand product knowledge. |\n| User control and freedom | 2 | Clear selection and Undo exist, but individual selection entry is hidden and the palette lacks a visible close control. |\n| Consistency and standards | 3 | Stable glyphs, tonal hierarchy and 48px controls mostly hold; the bulk primary action alone drops to 40px. |\n| Error prevention | 2 | “Mark N done” states quantity, but loaded-only scope is hidden and a quick swipe can commit without the armed cue. |\n| Recognition rather than recall | 2 | Long-press selection, configurable swipe meanings, and recovery through another destination rely on remembered behavior. |\n| Flexibility and efficiency | 3 | Swipe, long-press, explicit bulk setters and a searchable command list support fast processing; the picker is verbose. |\n| Aesthetic and minimalist design | 3 | Rows are quiet and legible; repeated floating containers and long reminder dates compete with the mail. |\n| Recognize, diagnose, recover from errors | 2 | Failure/unknown/partial outcomes are honest; “see Activity” and expired-Undo text do not themselves take the user to recovery. |\n| Help and documentation | 2 | Local-only and timezone explanations help; the primary phone selection gesture has no visible or accessible local teaching cue. |\n\n## 3. Cognitive load — eight checks\n\n| Check | Result | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Single focus | Partial failure | Inbox focuses on mail; selection-with-notice.png asks the user to reconcile a previous action, current selection, and persistent navigation. |\n| Chunking ≤4 per group | Fail | Drawer has nine destination/utility entries in one visual run; palette’s Conversation group has eight actions. |\n| Grouping | Partial failure | Drawer divider is inserted by numeric index, so available capabilities determine its semantic grouping. In drawer.png it does not separate mail from utilities. |\n| Visual hierarchy | Partial failure | Rows pass through weight and tone; reminder options give secondary full dates nearly the same visual authority as their labels. |\n| One thing at a time | Fail | Reminder sheet exposes time entry, four suggestions, return condition and scheduling together, after several lines of setup text. |\n| ≤4 visible options per decision | Fail | Drawer: nine entries. Palette: eight Conversation actions plus Select all loaded visible. Selection: four task actions plus Clear; navigation adds two more exits. Reminder time: four presets plus custom entry = five paths. |\n| Working memory | Fail | Users must remember how to enter selection, that Select all means loaded rows only, and that Undo has an unseen deadline. |\n| Progressive disclosure | Mixed | Reminder subject details and command search are useful disclosure; blank-query palette exposes a large command catalogue, and the picker repeats timezone detail instead of disclosing it once. |\n\nThese are decision-load flags, not a claim that every list must have four rows. Mail rows are content to scan, not nine unrelated commands. The drawer and blank-query palette need semantic groups; they do not need arbitrary pagination into groups of four.\n\n## 4. Emotional journey\n\n**Arrival:** calm and competent. Sender names and unread weight make the first scan straightforward. Android confirms this is not merely a browser rendering success. The conspicuously empty header and two lower pills make the product feel more ceremonial than fast, but do not block triage.\n\n**Peak:** the armed done swipe is the most decisive moment. Lavender fill, dark check, and stronger label make commitment tangible. However, the velocity shortcut can bypass that reassurance; the visual promise and actual trigger need to agree.\n\n**Valley:** selection shifts every text column 48px right and compresses subjects while a large action panel arrives. A previous-action toast above that panel makes “what I selected” and “what I just did” adjacent competing facts. The user should never have to audit the interface before auditing their selection.\n\n**Bulk mark done:** quantity in “Mark N done” is valuable reassurance. It is not a preview of which loaded conversations are affected, and the loaded-only qualification is hidden from sight. The handler immediately submits the group. For multiple conversations, show a lightweight scope review before submission, retaining the current exact outcome ledger afterward.\n\n**Undo expiry / ending:** confirmation is reassuring; a disappearing Undo followed by a persistent “Undo expired” instruction makes a successful action end on lost capability. Preserve pending/failed/unknown states, but let a settled success finish quietly and offer a direct recovery destination when the user asks for details. Do not replace truthful status with optimistic “Done.” Expiry persistence is inherited behavior, not a newly introduced mobile defect.\n\n## 5. Specific strengths\n\n- **The row anatomy works.** Sender at 16px, subject at 15px, preview at 14px, and 13px date create a restrained scan ladder; unread uses both weight and a dot. The Android image demonstrates fallback email senders and long subjects without horizontal page spill. Evidence: inbox.png, android/inbox.png; [list.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:157).\n- **Commit states have material difference.** Done becomes solid lavender with dark text; Remind stays neutral. The source supplies short 120–160ms transitions and reduced-motion removal rather than gratuitous animation. Evidence: three swipe captures; [list.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:119), [useSwipeAction.ts](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:113). Timing is code-reviewed, not observed in motion.\n- **Truth is part of the interface.** “Accepted locally, not yet confirmed,” confirmed/failed/unknown distinctions, group outcome counts, and “Local to this device · never sent” give this product a credible voice. Evidence: inbox-undo-toast.png, drafts.png; [ActionNotice.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:193), [DraftsList.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/DraftsList.vue:295).\n\n## 6. Priority issues\n\n### P1 — Selection hides its entry and its scope\n\n**What:** Before selecting, `.row-select` is `display:none`; only selection mode reveals it. Long-press accepts touch/pen and waits 500ms. There is no local single-row selection button exposed to touch exploration. The palette’s Select all loaded is a global workaround, not an equivalent individual-row entry. Once active, the bar says “Select all (18)” and hides “18 loaded.” Its primary action is only 40px high. For bulk archive, the handler submits immediately rather than presenting a scope review.\n\n**Why it matters:** Sam loses the direct accessible checkbox route; Casey must discover an invisible gesture; Alex can mistake a loaded subset for the mailbox. Count alone does not satisfy the product’s “preview before power” principle.\n\n**Fix:** Add a visible 48×48px “Select conversations” control to the list context, entering selection without selecting everything; reveal existing labelled checkboxes in that mode. Change compact scope to “1 of 18 loaded selected” and the secondary action to “Select 18 loaded.” Set `.selection-row-primary > button:not([data-test=\"selection-clear\"])` to `min-height:48px`. For count >1, intercept archive with a sheet stating “Mark 18 loaded conversations done?” and “Unloaded conversations stay unchanged,” with reviewable sender/subject identities and a “Mark 18 done” confirmation. Keep the single-item path fast.\n\n**Evidence:** selection.png, android/selection.png; [list.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:248), [SelectionBar.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/SelectionBar.vue:68), [useLongPressSelection.ts](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useLongPressSelection.ts:17), [App.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/App.vue:205).\n\n### P1 — The bottom stack consumes reading space without reserving its full height\n\n**What:** Selection plus a notice builds a substantial floating stack above navigation. `.list-dock` is absolute; scrollers clear only `--mobile-bottom-clearance`, which includes navigation and an optional context pill, not the dock’s variable height. Thus an end-of-list row can stop underneath the selection/notice stack. That last-row consequence is inferred from the CSS; there is no supplied selection-at-end capture. The ordinary inbox-scrolled-end.png does clear the final row and should not be reported as broken. Notices also keep expiry instructions in the same floating surface, without an in-notice recovery link or rendered dismiss control.\n\n**Why it matters:** The controls cover the information needed to use them confidently. Long subjects and inherited expiry copy make that coverage worse. A settled success should not become permanent visual debt.\n\n**Fix:** Put the dock in the compact flex layout above navigation so the list viewport shrinks by its actual height, or measure `.list-dock` with ResizeObserver and add `--mobile-dock-height` plus an 8px gap to both scroll end padding and scroll padding. Keep the selected row in the resulting visible area. Retain truthful pending/problem states. For settled notices, add Dismiss, show Undo with its remaining eligibility, and after expiry collapse to a short outcome plus a direct “View in Done” / “View Activity” action appropriate to single/group recovery. Do not automatically dismiss unresolved acceptance.\n\n**Evidence:** selection-with-notice.png, inbox-undo-toast.png; [shell.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/shell.css:204), [tokens.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/tokens.css:22), [notices.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/notices.css:2), [ActionNotice.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:248).\n\n### P1 — Swipe commitment and the armed cue use different rules\n\n**What:** Armed appearance begins at 35% of row width. Release also commits at just 64px if velocity exceeds 0.5px/ms. On a 412px row, that permits commitment far below the approximately 144px visual threshold. In swipe-done.png the narrow reveal clips “Mark done”; its trailing alignment and overflow clipping are real CSS behavior, not harness chrome. The glyph is selected by direction rather than configured action, so swapping preferences can pair the wrong icon with the correct label.\n\n**Why it matters:** A fast gesture can act before the interface has visibly promised to act. Wrong glyphs teach the wrong gesture when preferences change.\n\n**Fix:** Use one commit predicate for both `armed` and release; safest here is the documented 35% distance rule, removing the hidden velocity exception. Derive the glyph from the resolved action: reminder → clock; done → archive/restore according to context. In `.row-swipe-underlay`, reveal a 24px action glyph first and show the full label only when at least 128px is exposed; keep its leading edge intact. Keep the neutral/armed color difference and reduced-motion behavior.\n\n**Evidence:** swipe-done.png, swipe-done-armed.png, swipe-remind-armed.png; [useSwipeAction.ts](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:87), [InboxList.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:736), [list.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:90).\n\n### P2 — Reminder scheduling spends its hierarchy on explanation\n\n**What:** The sheet repeats timezone information in the introduction and each verbose timestamp. Dates wrap over three lines, while explanatory text, conversation identity, option labels, condition and primary action share nearly one type size. The sheet dominates the screen before a keyboard appears. No supplied keyboard-open picker capture establishes actual keyboard obstruction, so that is an unverified risk, not a finding.\n\n**Why it matters:** “Tomorrow morning” becomes a reading task. The action feels slower and more consequential than its underlying choice.\n\n**Fix:** In compact mode, render preset rows as a two-line grid: label at 15px/600, secondary date at 13px/400, start-aligned, `min-height:56px; gap:4px`. Use “Wed 18 Nov · 8:00 AM”; state the device timezone once as “Times in UTC” with an expandable full zone/date explanation. Preserve the exact instant in `<time datetime>` and an accessible full-date label. Keep sender and one truncated subject above time entry, with the existing disclosure for the full subject. Put condition under a closed “Return if no reply” disclosure; retain an explicit 48px Schedule reminder action after time selection.\n\n**Evidence:** reminder-picker.png; [ReminderPicker.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:138), [ReminderPicker.vue formatting](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:158), [pages.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/pages.css:204).\n\n### P2 — Drawer and palette flatten different kinds of decisions\n\n**What:** The drawer mixes mail views and utilities in one run. Its separator uses `index === 8`, so capability filtering changes what it separates. The blank-query palette lists eight Conversation actions, including both Star/Unstar and read/unread setters. It has no visible close button; escape/backdrop/Android Back are exit routes, but the keyboard footer is hidden on compact.\n\n**Why it matters:** Navigation becomes a search problem, and Commands feels like a desktop catalogue. A distracted user opening it accidentally lacks an obvious local exit.\n\n**Fix:** Group drawer entries by stable IDs, not rendered indices: Mail (Inbox/Starred/Drafts/Done), Follow-up (Reminders and the existing saved split when available), Utilities (Provider mail/Activity/Account/Settings), then Commands. Use 13px section captions and 8px group gaps, retaining 48px rows. In the compact palette, show four relevant shortcuts for an empty query plus “All commands”; search must still return all matching commands. Preserve explicit read/unread setters. Add a 48×48px Close button beside the query without shrinking the input below usable width.\n\n**Evidence:** drawer.png, palette.png; [MobileDrawer.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/mobile/MobileDrawer.vue:102), [ActionOverlay.vue](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionOverlay.vue:90), [pages.css](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/pages.css:577).\n\nNo P0 is substantiated by these static captures and source inspection.\n\n## 7. Persona red flags\n\n- **Alex — impatient power user:** `.picker small` turns preset dates into paragraphs; `.compact-command-overlay` exposes a large blank-query inventory; the hidden velocity rule makes learned swipe feedback unreliable. “Select all (N)” conceals the loaded-scope distinction Alex is most likely to exploit at speed.\n- **Casey — one-handed, distracted:** `mobile-menu` and `mobile-compose` sit at opposite top corners; most destinations require the upper-left menu. The two bottom icons are reachable, but their visible labels are absent. `.selection-row-primary` gives the consequential action a smaller target than Clear; the notice/selection stack obscures nearby mail. A 500ms long-press offers no visible entry cue.\n- **Sam — screen reader / low vision:** `.row-select { display:none }` removes individual selection controls until a pointer gesture or global-selection workaround starts the mode. The 48px checkbox targets and accessible labels are good once exposed. `.selection-row-secondary` is nowrap, and its three labels plus padding have no large-text reflow rule: overflow at enlarged text is a code-based risk, not something these default-size images prove. The palette’s single combobox tab stop and lack of a close button need real TalkBack testing; source semantics alone do not establish successful operation.\n\n## 8. Minor observations\n\n- The paired top-button rings and stacked bottom pills are the most category-interchangeable part of the design. Reduce framing before adding brand flourishes. On Inbox, the lone context tab should earn its own 44px container only when another supported split is present.\n- The bottom Mail icon repeats the Inbox context above it; in Drafts neither bottom icon represents the current destination. The visible Drafts heading prevents total loss of orientation, but the navigation model is semantically uneven. Evidence: drafts.png; MobileBottomBar’s `inboxOrSaved` current-state gate.\n- Drafts has both the header pencil and a full-width New draft button. The empty state is usefully factual, but one reachable primary creation control would be cleaner. Populated draft rows and discard recovery were code-read, not visually validated: drafts.png is empty.\n- Selection shifts text from a 20px to a 68px start inset, reducing scan continuity. Keep checkbox entry explicit and test retaining a stable text gutter during mode changes.\n- Dates and previews are readable in the supplied captures; do not invent contrast failures from the muted palette. No formal contrast or large-text measurement was performed in Assessment A.\n- The lavender outline on the drawer’s current Inbox row matches its programmatically assigned focus. Do not count the screenshot’s focus treatment as a second persistent selection border.\n- CSS supplies a drawer transform transition, but the drawer mounts already at `translateX(0)` under `v-if`. An entrance slide is not established by that declaration alone; inspect actual motion before claiming it exists. Row and sheet timings likewise need device observation for perceived quality.\n\n## Provocative questions\n\n1. If this is a precision instrument, why is the consequential scope word—“loaded”—the part removed from sight on a phone?\n2. Should an otherwise successful triage session end with a persistent explanation of the recovery window the user just lost?\n3. If the phone’s main job is scan → decide → act, what do the two permanent bottom containers contribute on an Inbox with only one split and two navigation icons?\n","numLines":136,"startLine":1,"totalLines":136}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a1c3754f-34fb-49f9-b0c0-644c8e3fd2b1","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"t3-code","cwd":"/home/exedev/email-os","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","version":"2.1.326","gitBranch":"main","slug":"cached-bubbling-meadow"}
