{"type":"tools-used","tools":["Bash","Read","ToolSearch","mcp__t3-code__orchestrator_capabilities","mcp__t3-code__t3_thread_list","mcp__t3-code__device_list","mcp__t3-code__t3_thread_launch","Write","mcp__t3-code__t3_thread_wait","mcp__t3-code__t3_thread_read","mcp__t3-code__t3_thread_send","mcp__t3-code__t3_background_wait","mcp__t3-code__device_open","mcp__t3-code__link_pull_request","Skill","Agent","AskUserQuestion","Edit","mcp__t3-code__device_screenshot","mcp__t3-code__delegate_task","mcp__t3-code__send_to_user"],"sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b"}
{"parentUuid":"f0df8dc8-545e-483e-bf1c-532158fb093b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch98oYRK1kBdvChnmS3WZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm accepting the fling commit as a normal mobile pattern; the real gap is that no \"armed\" cue appears. I could either set the armed state on a fling too, or leave fling as is, since mail apps do it that way. Scoring the two halves together: visibility looks like a 3 out of 4.\n\nI'm scoring the Match heuristic, which sits between 2 and 3. Reader icons being unlabeled weighs more under H6, so I'll keep Match at 3. Working through the per-item averages gave a 25.5 total, so I'm settling the ten items as whole numbers that add up, and I'll note that the two reviews used different models and splits, so the scores aren't directly comparable.\n\nI'm going to give the ninth item a 2 rather than a 3, since the draft-failure recovery is solid but the expired-undo path is weak, and that pulls it down. Rather than force the totals to match one figure, I'll just report the combined 25 out of 40 as the two reviewers' scores taken together, without adjusting for the fact that their models and splits differ. Next I'm settling on the top issues to raise, starting with the settled notice that keeps showing \"Undo expired\" over the reader, search, and selection views, which both reviewers flagged on their own.\n\nI'm working through the priority issues now. The first is the settled-success notice that keeps showing \"Undo expired\" over the reader, search, and selection views, which both reviewers flagged independently. Next I'm weighing the reader action bar's six unlabeled peers and the hidden, long-press-only selection entry, with the bulk confirm sheet as one option to consider.\n\nNext I'm noting the contact page lacks phone-friendly treatment, with small recent rows and copy buttons hidden until hover, which I've verified. The swipe issue is that the glyph follows direction rather than the configured action, and the fling commit lacks a cue below the armed threshold. Lower-priority items include the reminder picker verbosity, drawer grouping, clipped subjects, preview leading, dock clearance, and the palette's missing close button.\n\nI'm drafting the report for the user now, so I'll send it in a few parts to stay under the length limit, and then put the questions to them once they've read it.\n\nI'm preparing to deliver the report in chunks, sending each through the message tool, before saving the snapshot and asking the user my questions.\n\nI'm laying out the report in three chunks: the header and score table first, then what's working with the top issues, then the remaining issues, persona notes, and questions.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":404,"cache_read_input_tokens":273534,"output_tokens":3265,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2008},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":404,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3265,"cache_read_input_tokens":273534,"cache_creation_input_tokens":404,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":404},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":23209,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch98oUk3ZKfmdbB6zFo1T","requestRef":"b9d307fb-374b-4b05-bc7e-959893044b69","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"8eb4fa0c-1be6-4988-992a-76c61d4c31e7","timestamp":"2026-11-17T20:31:48.705Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","madeBy":"model","userType":"external","entrypoint":"t3-code","cwd":"/home/exedev/email-os","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","version":"2.1.326","gitBranch":"main","slug":"cached-bubbling-meadow"}
{"parentUuid":"8eb4fa0c-1be6-4988-992a-76c61d4c31e7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch98oYRK1kBdvChnmS3WZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've verified the swipe threshold, icon, row heights, divider position, and subject truncation in code, and I'm writing the combined report next.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":404,"cache_read_input_tokens":273534,"output_tokens":3265,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2008},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":404,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3265,"cache_read_input_tokens":273534,"cache_creation_input_tokens":404,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":404},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":3,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch98oUk3ZKfmdbB6zFo1T","requestRef":"b9d307fb-374b-4b05-bc7e-959893044b69","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"67e18f80-5fa1-49a5-94a0-948d82355535","timestamp":"2026-11-17T20:31:48.718Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","madeBy":"model","userType":"external","entrypoint":"t3-code","cwd":"/home/exedev/email-os","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","version":"2.1.326","gitBranch":"main","slug":"cached-bubbling-meadow"}
{"parentUuid":"67e18f80-5fa1-49a5-94a0-948d82355535","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch98oYRK1kBdvChnmS3WZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TccHnZjCX5uSNRFCJ5uw6S","name":"mcp__t3-code__send_to_user","input":{"message":"Method: three isolated GPT-6.1-SOL sub-agents at high effort (A1: list, selection, swipes, drawer · A2: reader, editors, pages · B: detector). A1 and A2 never saw B's output or each other's.\n\n## Design Health Score: 25/40 (Acceptable)\n\n| # | Heuristic | Score | Key issue |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Visibility of system status | 3 | Exact outcome states; Undo shows no deadline |\n| 2 | Match with real world | 3 | Row order is natural; \"Provider mail\" and \"operations\" are product words |\n| 3 | User control and freedom | 3 | Back and Undo work everywhere now; a settled notice can't be dismissed |\n| 4 | Consistency and standards | 2 | Contact uses 24px desktop targets; bulk action button is 40px |\n| 5 | Error prevention | 2 | A fast swipe commits before the armed cue shows; \"loaded\" scope is hidden |\n| 6 | Recognition rather than recall | 2 | Reader icons are unlabelled; selection only by a hidden long-press |\n| 7 | Flexibility and efficiency | 3 | Swipe, long-press, bulk actions and commands all present |\n| 8 | Aesthetic and minimalist | 3 | Quiet rows; stacked floating bars compete with mail |\n| 9 | Error recovery | 2 | Draft failures are excellent; \"Undo expired\" names a fix but doesn't take you there |\n| 10 | Help and documentation | 2 | Good disclosures; touch meanings live in hidden labels |\n\nThe two halves scored 25 (list) and 26 (reader/pages). This is not a like-for-like comparison with the last run's 25/40. Last time four Sonnet reviewers looked mainly at the five issues we then fixed. These reviewers went deeper into areas nobody had touched. None of the old five came back as a priority issue.\n\n## Design specificity\nBoth reviewers say the product's identity lives in its behaviour, and that this works: \"Saved locally · never sent\", the incomplete-coverage notice in search, and \"Accepted locally, not yet confirmed\". The weaker part is the frame. Two round top buttons, a full-width pill holding one Inbox tab, and another holding two icons feel generic. A1's phrase: \"a carefully styled desktop action model fitted into a phone shell\".\n\n**Detector:** the code scan found nothing except one advisory (an 8px radius on the startup card). The rendered scan of the inbox at 412×915 found 22 hits:\n- 18 are one real issue: row previews use 1.25 line spacing, below the 1.3 minimum.\n- 4 are false positives: text that sits under the floating bars only at that scroll position.\n- Neither design reviewer caught the line spacing.\n\nNo overlay was possible because this is a headless machine. Only the inbox could be scanned; other states can't be reached by URL.\n\n## Overall impression\nTriage on the phone is calm and trustworthy. The biggest opportunity: finished actions should end quietly, and the important controls should say what they do."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":404,"cache_read_input_tokens":273534,"output_tokens":3265,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2008},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":404,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3265,"cache_read_input_tokens":273534,"cache_creation_input_tokens":404,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":404},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":9332,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01TccHnZjCX5uSNRFCJ5uw6S":{"message":"Method: three isolated GPT-6.1-SOL sub-agents at high effort (A1: list, selection, swipes, drawer · A2: reader, editors, pages · B: detector). A1 and A2 never saw B's output or each other's.\n\n## Design Health Score: 25/40 (Acceptable)\n\n| # | Heuristic | Score | Key issue |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Visibility of system status | 3 | Exact outcome states; Undo shows no deadline |\n| 2 | Match with real world | 3 | Row order is natural; \"Provider mail\" and \"operations\" are product words |\n| 3 | User control and freedom | 3 | Back and Undo work everywhere now; a settled notice can't be dismissed |\n| 4 | Consistency and standards | 2 | Contact uses 24px desktop targets; bulk action button is 40px |\n| 5 | Error prevention | 2 | A fast swipe commits before the armed cue shows; \"loaded\" scope is hidden |\n| 6 | Recognition rather than recall | 2 | Reader icons are unlabelled; selection only by a hidden long-press |\n| 7 | Flexibility and efficiency | 3 | Swipe, long-press, bulk actions and commands all present |\n| 8 | Aesthetic and minimalist | 3 | Quiet rows; stacked floating bars compete with mail |\n| 9 | Error recovery | 2 | Draft failures are excellent; \"Undo expired\" names a fix but doesn't take you there |\n| 10 | Help and documentation | 2 | Good disclosures; touch meanings live in hidden labels |\n\nThe two halves scored 25 (list) and 26 (reader/pages). This is not a like-for-like comparison with the last run's 25/40. Last time four Sonnet reviewers looked mainly at the five issues we then fixed. These reviewers went deeper into areas nobody had touched. None of the old five came back as a priority issue.\n\n## Design specificity\nBoth reviewers say the product's identity lives in its behaviour, and that this works: \"Saved locally · never sent\", the incomplete-coverage notice in search, and \"Accepted locally, not yet confirmed\". The weaker part is the frame. Two round top buttons, a full-width pill holding one Inbox tab, and another holding two icons feel generic. A1's phrase: \"a carefully styled desktop action model fitted into a phone shell\".\n\n**Detector:** the code scan found nothing except one advisory (an 8px radius on the startup card). The rendered scan of the inbox at 412×915 found 22 hits:\n- 18 are one real issue: row previews use 1.25 line spacing, below the 1.3 minimum.\n- 4 are false positives: text that sits under the floating bars only at that scroll position.\n- Neither design reviewer caught the line spacing.\n\nNo overlay was possible because this is a headless machine. Only the inbox could be scanned; other states can't be reached by URL.\n\n## Overall impression\nTriage on the phone is calm and trustworthy. The biggest opportunity: finished actions should end quietly, and the important controls should say what they do."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Ch98oUk3ZKfmdbB6zFo1T","requestRef":"b9d307fb-374b-4b05-bc7e-959893044b69","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"ba8dff72-3271-4747-817e-90166157913d","timestamp":"2026-11-17T20:31:58.050Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","madeBy":"model","userType":"external","entrypoint":"t3-code","cwd":"/home/exedev/email-os","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","version":"2.1.326","gitBranch":"main","slug":"cached-bubbling-meadow"}
{"parentUuid":"ba8dff72-3271-4747-817e-90166157913d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9f6f758c-81e7-4f81-8936-fdb94b5d7a51","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TccHnZjCX5uSNRFCJ5uw6S","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"sentMessage\":\"Method: three isolated GPT-6.1-SOL sub-agents at high effort (A1: list, selection, swipes, drawer · A2: reader, editors, pages · B: detector). A1 and A2 never saw B's output or each other's.\\n\\n## Design Health Score: 25/40 (Acceptable)\\n\\n| # | Heuristic | Score | Key issue |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Visibility of system status | 3 | Exact outcome states; Undo shows no deadline |\\n| 2 | Match with real world | 3 | Row order is natural; \\\"Provider mail\\\" and \\\"operations\\\" are product words |\\n| 3 | User control and freedom | 3 | Back and Undo work everywhere now; a settled notice can't be dismissed |\\n| 4 | Consistency and standards | 2 | Contact uses 24px desktop targets; bulk action button is 40px |\\n| 5 | Error prevention | 2 | A fast swipe commits before the armed cue shows; \\\"loaded\\\" scope is hidden |\\n| 6 | Recognition rather than recall | 2 | Reader icons are unlabelled; selection only by a hidden long-press |\\n| 7 | Flexibility and efficiency | 3 | Swipe, long-press, bulk actions and commands all present |\\n| 8 | Aesthetic and minimalist | 3 | Quiet rows; stacked floating bars compete with mail |\\n| 9 | Error recovery | 2 | Draft failures are excellent; \\\"Undo expired\\\" names a fix but doesn't take you there |\\n| 10 | Help and documentation | 2 | Good disclosures; touch meanings live in hidden labels |\\n\\nThe two halves scored 25 (list) and 26 (reader/pages). This is not a like-for-like comparison with the last run's 25/40. Last time four Sonnet reviewers looked mainly at the five issues we then fixed. These reviewers went deeper into areas nobody had touched. None of the old five came back as a priority issue.\\n\\n## Design specificity\\nBoth reviewers say the product's identity lives in its behaviour, and that this works: \\\"Saved locally · never sent\\\", the incomplete-coverage notice in search, and \\\"Accepted locally, not yet confirmed\\\". The weaker part is the frame. Two round top buttons, a full-width pill holding one Inbox tab, and another holding two icons feel generic. A1's phrase: \\\"a carefully styled desktop action model fitted into a phone shell\\\".\\n\\n**Detector:** the code scan found nothing except one advisory (an 8px radius on the startup card). The rendered scan of the inbox at 412×915 found 22 hits:\\n- 18 are one real issue: row previews use 1.25 line spacing, below the 1.3 minimum.\\n- 4 are false positives: text that sits under the floating bars only at that scroll position.\\n- Neither design reviewer caught the line spacing.\\n\\nNo overlay was possible because this is a headless machine. Only the inbox could be scanned; other states can't be reached by URL.\\n\\n## Overall impression\\nTriage on the phone is calm and trustworthy. The biggest opportunity: finished actions should end quietly, and the important controls should say what they do.\",\"message\":\"Shown to the user. Don't repeat it.\"}"}]},"uuid":"7afcbbbf-a698-415e-b650-a01f150b1f73","timestamp":"2026-11-17T20:31:58.090Z","toolUseResult":"{\"sentMessage\":\"Method: three isolated GPT-6.1-SOL sub-agents at high effort (A1: list, selection, swipes, drawer · A2: reader, editors, pages · B: detector). A1 and A2 never saw B's output or each other's.\\n\\n## Design Health Score: 25/40 (Acceptable)\\n\\n| # | Heuristic | Score | Key issue |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Visibility of system status | 3 | Exact outcome states; Undo shows no deadline |\\n| 2 | Match with real world | 3 | Row order is natural; \\\"Provider mail\\\" and \\\"operations\\\" are product words |\\n| 3 | User control and freedom | 3 | Back and Undo work everywhere now; a settled notice can't be dismissed |\\n| 4 | Consistency and standards | 2 | Contact uses 24px desktop targets; bulk action button is 40px |\\n| 5 | Error prevention | 2 | A fast swipe commits before the armed cue shows; \\\"loaded\\\" scope is hidden |\\n| 6 | Recognition rather than recall | 2 | Reader icons are unlabelled; selection only by a hidden long-press |\\n| 7 | Flexibility and efficiency | 3 | Swipe, long-press, bulk actions and commands all present |\\n| 8 | Aesthetic and minimalist | 3 | Quiet rows; stacked floating bars compete with mail |\\n| 9 | Error recovery | 2 | Draft failures are excellent; \\\"Undo expired\\\" names a fix but doesn't take you there |\\n| 10 | Help and documentation | 2 | Good disclosures; touch meanings live in hidden labels |\\n\\nThe two halves scored 25 (list) and 26 (reader/pages). This is not a like-for-like comparison with the last run's 25/40. Last time four Sonnet reviewers looked mainly at the five issues we then fixed. These reviewers went deeper into areas nobody had touched. None of the old five came back as a priority issue.\\n\\n## Design specificity\\nBoth reviewers say the product's identity lives in its behaviour, and that this works: \\\"Saved locally · never sent\\\", the incomplete-coverage notice in search, and \\\"Accepted locally, not yet confirmed\\\". The weaker part is the frame. Two round top buttons, a full-width pill holding one Inbox tab, and another holding two icons feel generic. A1's phrase: \\\"a carefully styled desktop action model fitted into a phone shell\\\".\\n\\n**Detector:** the code scan found nothing except one advisory (an 8px radius on the startup card). The rendered scan of the inbox at 412×915 found 22 hits:\\n- 18 are one real issue: row previews use 1.25 line spacing, below the 1.3 minimum.\\n- 4 are false positives: text that sits under the floating bars only at that scroll position.\\n- Neither design reviewer caught the line spacing.\\n\\nNo overlay was possible because this is a headless machine. Only the inbox could be scanned; other states can't be reached by URL.\\n\\n## Overall impression\\nTriage on the phone is calm and trustworthy. The biggest opportunity: finished actions should end quietly, and the important controls should say what they do.\",\"message\":\"Shown to the user. Don't repeat it.\"}","mcpMeta":{"_meta":{"io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo":{"name":"T3 Code","version":"0.1.10"}},"structuredContent":{"sentMessage":"Method: three isolated GPT-6.1-SOL sub-agents at high effort (A1: list, selection, swipes, drawer · A2: reader, editors, pages · B: detector). A1 and A2 never saw B's output or each other's.\n\n## Design Health Score: 25/40 (Acceptable)\n\n| # | Heuristic | Score | Key issue |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Visibility of system status | 3 | Exact outcome states; Undo shows no deadline |\n| 2 | Match with real world | 3 | Row order is natural; \"Provider mail\" and \"operations\" are product words |\n| 3 | User control and freedom | 3 | Back and Undo work everywhere now; a settled notice can't be dismissed |\n| 4 | Consistency and standards | 2 | Contact uses 24px desktop targets; bulk action button is 40px |\n| 5 | Error prevention | 2 | A fast swipe commits before the armed cue shows; \"loaded\" scope is hidden |\n| 6 | Recognition rather than recall | 2 | Reader icons are unlabelled; selection only by a hidden long-press |\n| 7 | Flexibility and efficiency | 3 | Swipe, long-press, bulk actions and commands all present |\n| 8 | Aesthetic and minimalist | 3 | Quiet rows; stacked floating bars compete with mail |\n| 9 | Error recovery | 2 | Draft failures are excellent; \"Undo expired\" names a fix but doesn't take you there |\n| 10 | Help and documentation | 2 | Good disclosures; touch meanings live in hidden labels |\n\nThe two halves scored 25 (list) and 26 (reader/pages). This is not a like-for-like comparison with the last run's 25/40. Last time four Sonnet reviewers looked mainly at the five issues we then fixed. These reviewers went deeper into areas nobody had touched. None of the old five came back as a priority issue.\n\n## Design specificity\nBoth reviewers say the product's identity lives in its behaviour, and that this works: \"Saved locally · never sent\", the incomplete-coverage notice in search, and \"Accepted locally, not yet confirmed\". The weaker part is the frame. Two round top buttons, a full-width pill holding one Inbox tab, and another holding two icons feel generic. A1's phrase: \"a carefully styled desktop action model fitted into a phone shell\".\n\n**Detector:** the code scan found nothing except one advisory (an 8px radius on the startup card). The rendered scan of the inbox at 412×915 found 22 hits:\n- 18 are one real issue: row previews use 1.25 line spacing, below the 1.3 minimum.\n- 4 are false positives: text that sits under the floating bars only at that scroll position.\n- Neither design reviewer caught the line spacing.\n\nNo overlay was possible because this is a headless machine. Only the inbox could be scanned; other states can't be reached by URL.\n\n## Overall impression\nTriage on the phone is calm and trustworthy. The biggest opportunity: finished actions should end quietly, and the important controls should say what they do.","message":"Shown to the user. Don't repeat it."}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ba8dff72-3271-4747-817e-90166157913d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"t3-code","cwd":"/home/exedev/email-os","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","version":"2.1.326","gitBranch":"main","slug":"cached-bubbling-meadow"}
