{"parentUuid":"87ddcdbe-4fd7-432b-abf2-69fede70c33c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch9EESSR1QnS94fPzvc7E","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DyWMEh5JG4CCrW26Y2Pui2","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/A1-list.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":767,"cache_read_input_tokens":440438,"output_tokens":76,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":767,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":76,"cache_read_input_tokens":440438,"cache_creation_input_tokens":767,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":767},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":573,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01DyWMEh5JG4CCrW26Y2Pui2":{"file_path":"/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/A1-list.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch9EEMFsR76F13VNsaVnU","requestRef":"ef4b6b24-86eb-4d83-a728-50bf1f326b4a","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"c6a60ffa-1a26-4e35-b654-c3172d677a3e","timestamp":"2026-11-17T21:42:37.911Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","madeBy":"model","userType":"external","entrypoint":"t3-code","cwd":"/home/exedev/email-os","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","version":"2.1.326","gitBranch":"main","slug":"cached-bubbling-meadow"}
{"parentUuid":"c6a60ffa-1a26-4e35-b654-c3172d677a3e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"78913aa5-1136-493b-a758-3f044dcad330","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DyWMEh5JG4CCrW26Y2Pui2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Assessment A · Phone mail lists\n2\t\n3\tReviewed all 19 assigned captures against commit `ac939a3`. Scores cover the assigned compact surfaces. The browser account strip and yellow Preview label are harness chrome. Motion and accessibility findings are based on stills and source review, with no live gesture or TalkBack testing.\n4\t\n5\t## 1. Design specificity\n6\t\n7\tEmail OS has a recognizable point of view where triage gets serious. “Mark done,” the adjacent Done destination, explicit selection counts, and receipts that distinguish acceptance from confirmation describe a system built to get through an inbox with confidence. The warm charcoal, thin rules, orchid unread marks and lavender focus cues make for a quiet, legible list, although the system type, slide-out drawer and floating pills could belong to many mail apps. The phone adaptation loses some of the product’s precision exactly when it matters. A fast swipe can commit before its verb appears, and an accurate receipt disappears when its recovery control expires. Fixing those moments would give the phone experience more identity than adding visual ornament.\n8\t\n9\t## 2. Nielsen heuristics · 25/40\n10\t\n11\t| Heuristic | Score | Key issue |\n12\t|---|---:|---|\n13\t| Visibility of system status | 3 | Receipts identify the action and target, but expiry silently clears a settled result. |\n14\t| Match between system and the real world | 3 | Done and Remind fit the work. “Select 14 loaded” makes users interpret a cache concept. |\n15\t| User control and freedom | 2 | Clear, Back and Undo provide exits, but selection still accepts single-row swipes and Undo expires quickly. |\n16\t| Consistency and standards | 3 | Action verbs mostly carry across surfaces, but the Inbox name sits in a bottom pill while Drafts gets a header title. |\n17\t| Error prevention | 2 | The counted bulk verb makes scope visible. The swipe fast path can commit before its action label appears. |\n18\t| Recognition rather than recall | 2 | The command palette offers search, but row actions and the target of an unscoped command are hard to recognize from the resting list. |\n19\t| Flexibility and efficiency of use | 3 | Swipes, long-press and a searchable palette support frequent work. Reaching Commands by touch takes a trip through Destinations. |\n20\t| Aesthetic and minimalist design | 3 | Rows are restrained and readable. The duplicate Inbox affordances take a full extra row of space in the captured single-split state. |\n21\t| Help users recognize, diagnose and recover from errors | 3 | Failure and uncertainty have distinct copy and can lead to Activity, but the clean expiry path hides its recovery guidance. |\n22\t| Help and documentation | 1 | The reminder input gives examples and settings expose swipe preferences, but the phone list offers little in-context help for gestures or loaded scope. |\n23\t\n24\t## 3. Cognitive load · 6 of 8 fail somewhere in scope\n25\t\n26\t| Check | Assessment |\n27\t|---|---|\n28\t| Single focus | **Pass.** Resting Inbox is clearly for scanning mail. Sender, subject, preview and date have predictable positions, and the sheets dim the underlying list. |\n29\t| Chunking, up to four per group | **Fail.** The drawer opens as nine nearly equal choices. The palette’s Conversation group alone shows eight commands. |\n30\t| Grouping | **Fail.** Month captions and the two selection rows group well. The drawer’s positional separator fails to distinguish mail destinations from utilities in the captured fixture. |\n31\t| Visual hierarchy | **Fail.** Strong within a row. Across the screen, the Inbox title lives below the messages and the reminder’s full timestamp strings weigh as much as the choices they describe. |\n32\t| One thing at a time | **Fail.** Selection implies “choose a set,” but swipe can act on a single row beneath that mode. A prior receipt can compete with the new batch action. |\n33\t| Up to four visible options per decision | **Fail.** Drawer has **9** destinations/actions. Palette shows **9** executable rows, including **8** under Conversation. Populated Inbox selection has **5** controls including exit, or **6** when the previous action’s Undo is present. The reminder timing decision has **5** routes: four presets and custom text. Its return condition is a separate, two-option decision. |\n34\t| Working memory | **Fail.** After expiry, the user must remember the affected conversation and know where to look. In selection, the read-state indicator disappears just as Mark read and Mark unread appear. |\n35\t| Progressive disclosure | **Pass.** Selection setters appear after a row is selected. The reminder hides expanded subject detail, and commands stay behind an overlay. The timing of the swipe label is a separate prevention failure. |\n36\t\n37\tThe drawer separator is tied to a row index, while available destinations vary. The palette has category labels and search, which help once the user knows what to look for. [browser/drawer.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/drawer.png) · [browser/palette.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/palette.png) · [browser/selection-with-notice.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/selection-with-notice.png) · [browser/reminder-picker.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/reminder-picker.png) · [src/components/mobile/MobileDrawer.vue:102](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/mobile/MobileDrawer.vue:102)\n38\t\n39\t## 4. Emotional journey\n40\t\n41\t- **Peak.** The armed Done swipe gives the user a satisfying moment of intent through a solid lavender field, a larger check and a verb. The following receipt provides the stronger trust moment: the actual subject, “Confirmed,” a changed Inbox count and an in-reach Undo. Remind uses a different clock and neutral fill, and release opens a picker for review. [browser/swipe-done-armed.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/swipe-done-armed.png) · [browser/swipe-remind-armed.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/swipe-remind-armed.png) · [android/inbox-undo-toast.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox-undo-toast.png)\n42\t- **Valleys.** A fast swipe can skip the readable preview. Bulk selection is reassuring while the checked rows and count are visible, then “Mark N done” dispatches immediately and acceptance clears the selection. The existing group receipt can account for partial outcomes, but the transition removes the user’s view of the set before provider results settle. [android/selection-one.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/selection-one.png) · [src/App.vue:207](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/App.vue:207) · [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:135](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:135)\n43\t- **End.** Both captures end with a quieter inbox and a missing thread. After an interruption, silence leaves the user reconstructing where it went. The desirable end is a brief, explicit handoff from Undo to the real recovery path. [browser/inbox-after-undo-window.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/inbox-after-undo-window.png) · [android/inbox-after-undo-window.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox-after-undo-window.png)\n44\t\n45\t## 5. Strengths\n46\t\n47\t- **The rows carry the product.** The 16px sender, 15px subject, 14px preview and 13px metadata support fast scanning. Bold weight plus the unread dot gives a redundant signal. Android’s bare-mailbox sender and long literal fixture strings stay within the layout. [android/inbox.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox.png) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:165](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:165) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:273](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:273)\n48\t- **Selection distinguishes the set from focus.** Empty mode gives an instruction before showing setters. A filled checkbox and row fill identify selected rows, and “1 of 14 selected” plus “Mark 1 done” make the target count concrete. The visible checkbox is roughly 20px inside a 48px target, and tapping the row also toggles it. [android/selection-empty.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/selection-empty.png) · [android/selection-one.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/selection-one.png) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:271](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:271) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:96](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:96)\n49\t- **Receipts have a sound vocabulary.** Pending, confirmed, failed, and unknown outcomes have distinct words. Group receipts preserve per-outcome counts. Drafts applies the same plain-language discipline to “Local to this device · never sent.” [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:135](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:135) · [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:206](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:206) · [browser/drafts.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/drafts.png)\n50\t\n51\t## 6. Priority issues\n52\t\n53\t### P1 · Make a committing swipe predictable\n54\t\n55\t- **What.** A fast 64px gesture can commit at over 0.5px/ms, but the label first renders at 128px. The release recomputes average velocity, so a short swipe can also look armed during movement and cancel if the user pauses. Selection changes what a tap does, yet the pointer handlers still allow an explicit single-row action.\n56\t- **Why it matters.** The visual preview can lag behind a mutation. In a mode that foregrounds a selected set, the same gesture can remove a different conversation.\n57\t- **Fix.** In `useSwipeAction.ts`, start with a single distance rule, `Math.max(128, rowWidth * 0.35)`, and use it for both arming and release. In `InboxList.vue`, guard swipe start and commit with `!selecting.value` and cancel any pending or active swipe when selection begins. Keep release-to-cancel when the thumb moves back below the threshold and retain the reduced-motion path.\n58\t- **Evidence.** [browser/swipe-done.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/swipe-done.png) · [browser/swipe-done-armed.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/swipe-done-armed.png) · [src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:71](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:71) · [src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:116](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:116) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:366](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:366) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:750](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:750)\n59\t\n60\t### P1 · Give Undo a legible end and a handoff\n61\t\n62\t- **What.** Undo expires ten seconds after acceptance. The captured settled notice offers no visible time cue, then disappears. Compact rendering suppresses existing expiry guidance when a clean result settles.\n63\t- **Why it matters.** A distracted user returns to a changed inbox with no reminder of the subject or recovery route. A keyboard user focused on Undo can lose that control at expiry.\n64\t- **Fix.** Under the action subject, show “Undo for Ns” from the authoritative deadline. Keep the ticking number out of the live announcement and announce the expiry once. Retain the last confirmed Mark done receipt for another 8 seconds, or until the next action, with “Moved to Done” and a 48px “Open Done” button using the existing destination. For a bulk action, offer “View in Activity” and say recovery is per conversation. If Undo held focus, transfer focus to the replacement control. Keep the backend deadline authoritative.\n65\t- **Evidence.** [android/inbox-undo-toast.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox-undo-toast.png) · [android/inbox-after-undo-window.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox-after-undo-window.png) · [crates/mail-core/src/clock.rs:56](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/crates/mail-core/src/clock.rs:56) · [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:261](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:261) · [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:317](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:317)\n66\t\n67\t### P1 · Keep bulk targeting and read state legible\n68\t\n69\t- **What.** The checkbox replaces the read marker using `display: none`, removing its accessible state label with it. “Select N loaded” is terse but its touch-visible wording omits which mail is excluded. The largest batch takes two adjacent actions, Select all and Mark done, with no intervening review step.\n70\t- **Why it matters.** This mode is exactly where users need to distinguish read, unread and unknown and verify an unusually large change. Weight conveys some of that to sighted users only.\n71\t- **Fix.** Keep a distinct read-state cue by the sender in selection mode and preserve its accessible name. Give selected-row paperclips `var(--text-secondary)`. Use “Select all N shown” with a visible “Only conversations loaded in this list” line where more pages exist. For multi-row Mark done, make the bottom bar open an inline review state with “Move N conversations to Done?” and 48px “Keep selecting” and “Mark N done” controls. Keep the one-row path direct.\n72\t- **Evidence.** [android/inbox.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox.png) · [android/selection-one.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/selection-one.png) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:302](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:302) · [src/assets/style.css:1581](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/style.css:1581) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:783](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:783) · [src/components/SelectionBar.vue:55](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/SelectionBar.vue:55) · [src/App.vue:217](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/App.vue:217)\n73\t\n74\t### P2 · Give the list a real bottom edge\n75\t\n76\t- **What.** The floating split and navigation pills cut through rows at rest. The browser scroll-end capture proves that the ordinary last row does clear them. Selection plus a notice is taller, but both are absolutely positioned and the static clearance token accounts for the bottom bar and context pill only. The list’s resize observer relies on its actual height changing. Separately, a 48px split button sits inside the 44px overflow slot.\n77\t- **Why it matters.** Users scroll to reclaim text and checkboxes behind the controls. More text or wrapped actions can make the overlap worse. The split control also misses the intended 48px visible hit area vertically.\n78\t- **Fix.** Give compact lists a shared in-flow footer containing the notice, selection or context, and navigation, with `.central` filling and scrolling in the remaining space. Use 8px between footer sections and let selection and notice content wrap within the footer. Put “Inbox” in the header, and omit the standalone context pill when the Inbox is current and has no alternate split. Where the split pill offers a switch, give its overflow slot the full 48px and label the primary bottom buttons “Inbox” and “Search.”\n79\t- **Evidence.** [android/inbox.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox.png) · [browser/inbox-scrolled-end.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/inbox-scrolled-end.png) · [browser/selection-with-notice.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/selection-with-notice.png) · [src/assets/mobile/shell.css:120](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/shell.css:120) · [src/assets/mobile/shell.css:192](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/shell.css:192) · [src/assets/mobile/tokens.css:20](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/tokens.css:20) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:14](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:14) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:529](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:529)\n80\t\n81\t### P2 · Make the reminder sheet usable from the thumb\n82\t\n83\t- **What.** Three preset rows repeat the year, zone and offset, producing three lines per date in the capture. The dates also appear out of chronological order. Typing a custom time disables Schedule until the input’s separate Enter/submit path validates it. The compact rules hide the Enter hint.\n84\t- **Why it matters.** A frequent task becomes a long form. A user who types a valid time and taps the visible primary action can encounter a disabled button with no clear next step.\n85\t- **Fix.** Put the conversation identity and preset choices immediately under the heading. Sort dated presets by due time with Someday at the end. In compact preset rows show “Wed, Nov 18 · 8:00 AM” at 13px, state the device time zone once, and show the full instant and offset in the selected-time summary. Add a 48px “Use this time” control for edited input that calls the same validation as Enter, reports errors inline, and preserves the separate Schedule step and explicit return condition.\n86\t- **Evidence.** [browser/reminder-picker.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/reminder-picker.png) · [src/lib/reminder-time.ts:26](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/lib/reminder-time.ts:26) · [src/lib/reminder-timezone.ts:12](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/lib/reminder-timezone.ts:12) · [src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:54](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:54) · [src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:151](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:151) · [src/components/RemindersList.vue:193](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/RemindersList.vue:193) · [src/assets/mobile/pages.css:229](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/pages.css:229)\n87\t\n88\t## 7. Persona red flags\n89\t\n90\t- **Alex, impatient power user.** A fast flick can remove mail before the verb registers. In the palette, “Mark done” can act on the focused conversation without naming it in the captured command list. The dispatch scope is explicit for selections, and should be just as visible for a single target. [browser/palette.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/palette.png) · [src/App.vue:1426](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/App.vue:1426)\n91\t- **Casey, one-handed and distracted.** Menu and Select entry sit at the top. The drawer starts high and left, even though the bottom navigation is near the thumb. Returning to the screen after ten seconds turns recovery into a search for the missing conversation. [android/inbox.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox.png) · [browser/drawer.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/drawer.png) · [android/inbox-after-undo-window.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox-after-undo-window.png)\n92\t- **Sam, screen reader or low vision.** Native checkboxes, named navigation and a polite receipt region help. Selection removes the named read-state cue, and the attachment glyph’s muted token on the selected fill is about 2.75:1 before the unknown variant’s opacity. The disappearance of Undo and the visually clipped, keyboard-focusable checkbox outside selection deserve a TalkBack and hardware-keyboard pass. [src/components/InboxList.vue:772](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:772) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:257](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:257) · [src/assets/style.css:1581](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/style.css:1581) · [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:308](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:308)\n93\t\n94\t## 8. Minor observations and questions\n95\t\n96\t- Group drawer entries by purpose with stable boundaries, for example Inbox/Starred/Done, Drafts/Reminders, and utilities. Add an in-panel Close control. The scrim, Back/Escape and focus return already provide useful foundations. The CSS declares a 180ms transform transition, while the drawer mounts at `translateX(0)`; the stills cannot establish a slide-in. [browser/drawer.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/drawer.png) · [src/components/mobile/MobileDrawer.vue:50](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/mobile/MobileDrawer.vue:50) · [src/assets/mobile/drawer.css:16](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/drawer.css:16)\n97\t- In the palette, show the command target above the list and limit the initial touch shortlist to four relevant actions. Keep search across the full registry. Give touch users a visible Close control alongside outside tap and Escape. [browser/palette.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/palette.png) · [src/components/ActionOverlay.vue:48](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionOverlay.vue:48) · [src/components/ActionOverlay.vue:89](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionOverlay.vue:89)\n98\t- Put “No local drafts” ahead of the action in the empty state. Keep the labelled New draft button as the main action there. The top pencil currently duplicates it. [browser/drafts.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/drafts.png) · [src/components/DraftsList.vue:295](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/DraftsList.vue:295) · [src/components/mobile/MobileTopBar.vue:55](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/mobile/MobileTopBar.vue:55)\n99\t- The row sends a clear directional motion signal at 160ms and explicitly bypasses the exit animation under reduced motion. Keep the slide a cue for gesture acceptance and let the receipt communicate the provider outcome. [src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:123](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:123) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:453](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:453)\n100\t\n101\t1. If “Done” is where mail goes, why does the most important receipt teach “Confirmed” and leave the destination implicit?\n102\t2. If the first four commands are the product’s opinion about how to triage, should “Compose new local draft” lead when I opened Commands from Inbox?\n103\t3. Could someone who never learns a swipe still finish a one-handed triage session entirely from the list?\n104\t"}]},"uuid":"064f27fc-6514-4c77-83d5-a6f111a186e5","timestamp":"2026-11-17T21:42:37.955Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/A1-list.md","content":"# Assessment A · Phone mail lists\n\nReviewed all 19 assigned captures against commit `ac939a3`. Scores cover the assigned compact surfaces. The browser account strip and yellow Preview label are harness chrome. Motion and accessibility findings are based on stills and source review, with no live gesture or TalkBack testing.\n\n## 1. Design specificity\n\nEmail OS has a recognizable point of view where triage gets serious. “Mark done,” the adjacent Done destination, explicit selection counts, and receipts that distinguish acceptance from confirmation describe a system built to get through an inbox with confidence. The warm charcoal, thin rules, orchid unread marks and lavender focus cues make for a quiet, legible list, although the system type, slide-out drawer and floating pills could belong to many mail apps. The phone adaptation loses some of the product’s precision exactly when it matters. A fast swipe can commit before its verb appears, and an accurate receipt disappears when its recovery control expires. Fixing those moments would give the phone experience more identity than adding visual ornament.\n\n## 2. Nielsen heuristics · 25/40\n\n| Heuristic | Score | Key issue |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Visibility of system status | 3 | Receipts identify the action and target, but expiry silently clears a settled result. |\n| Match between system and the real world | 3 | Done and Remind fit the work. “Select 14 loaded” makes users interpret a cache concept. |\n| User control and freedom | 2 | Clear, Back and Undo provide exits, but selection still accepts single-row swipes and Undo expires quickly. |\n| Consistency and standards | 3 | Action verbs mostly carry across surfaces, but the Inbox name sits in a bottom pill while Drafts gets a header title. |\n| Error prevention | 2 | The counted bulk verb makes scope visible. The swipe fast path can commit before its action label appears. |\n| Recognition rather than recall | 2 | The command palette offers search, but row actions and the target of an unscoped command are hard to recognize from the resting list. |\n| Flexibility and efficiency of use | 3 | Swipes, long-press and a searchable palette support frequent work. Reaching Commands by touch takes a trip through Destinations. |\n| Aesthetic and minimalist design | 3 | Rows are restrained and readable. The duplicate Inbox affordances take a full extra row of space in the captured single-split state. |\n| Help users recognize, diagnose and recover from errors | 3 | Failure and uncertainty have distinct copy and can lead to Activity, but the clean expiry path hides its recovery guidance. |\n| Help and documentation | 1 | The reminder input gives examples and settings expose swipe preferences, but the phone list offers little in-context help for gestures or loaded scope. |\n\n## 3. Cognitive load · 6 of 8 fail somewhere in scope\n\n| Check | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| Single focus | **Pass.** Resting Inbox is clearly for scanning mail. Sender, subject, preview and date have predictable positions, and the sheets dim the underlying list. |\n| Chunking, up to four per group | **Fail.** The drawer opens as nine nearly equal choices. The palette’s Conversation group alone shows eight commands. |\n| Grouping | **Fail.** Month captions and the two selection rows group well. The drawer’s positional separator fails to distinguish mail destinations from utilities in the captured fixture. |\n| Visual hierarchy | **Fail.** Strong within a row. Across the screen, the Inbox title lives below the messages and the reminder’s full timestamp strings weigh as much as the choices they describe. |\n| One thing at a time | **Fail.** Selection implies “choose a set,” but swipe can act on a single row beneath that mode. A prior receipt can compete with the new batch action. |\n| Up to four visible options per decision | **Fail.** Drawer has **9** destinations/actions. Palette shows **9** executable rows, including **8** under Conversation. Populated Inbox selection has **5** controls including exit, or **6** when the previous action’s Undo is present. The reminder timing decision has **5** routes: four presets and custom text. Its return condition is a separate, two-option decision. |\n| Working memory | **Fail.** After expiry, the user must remember the affected conversation and know where to look. In selection, the read-state indicator disappears just as Mark read and Mark unread appear. |\n| Progressive disclosure | **Pass.** Selection setters appear after a row is selected. The reminder hides expanded subject detail, and commands stay behind an overlay. The timing of the swipe label is a separate prevention failure. |\n\nThe drawer separator is tied to a row index, while available destinations vary. The palette has category labels and search, which help once the user knows what to look for. [browser/drawer.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/drawer.png) · [browser/palette.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/palette.png) · [browser/selection-with-notice.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/selection-with-notice.png) · [browser/reminder-picker.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/reminder-picker.png) · [src/components/mobile/MobileDrawer.vue:102](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/mobile/MobileDrawer.vue:102)\n\n## 4. Emotional journey\n\n- **Peak.** The armed Done swipe gives the user a satisfying moment of intent through a solid lavender field, a larger check and a verb. The following receipt provides the stronger trust moment: the actual subject, “Confirmed,” a changed Inbox count and an in-reach Undo. Remind uses a different clock and neutral fill, and release opens a picker for review. [browser/swipe-done-armed.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/swipe-done-armed.png) · [browser/swipe-remind-armed.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/swipe-remind-armed.png) · [android/inbox-undo-toast.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox-undo-toast.png)\n- **Valleys.** A fast swipe can skip the readable preview. Bulk selection is reassuring while the checked rows and count are visible, then “Mark N done” dispatches immediately and acceptance clears the selection. The existing group receipt can account for partial outcomes, but the transition removes the user’s view of the set before provider results settle. [android/selection-one.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/selection-one.png) · [src/App.vue:207](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/App.vue:207) · [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:135](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:135)\n- **End.** Both captures end with a quieter inbox and a missing thread. After an interruption, silence leaves the user reconstructing where it went. The desirable end is a brief, explicit handoff from Undo to the real recovery path. [browser/inbox-after-undo-window.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/inbox-after-undo-window.png) · [android/inbox-after-undo-window.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox-after-undo-window.png)\n\n## 5. Strengths\n\n- **The rows carry the product.** The 16px sender, 15px subject, 14px preview and 13px metadata support fast scanning. Bold weight plus the unread dot gives a redundant signal. Android’s bare-mailbox sender and long literal fixture strings stay within the layout. [android/inbox.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox.png) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:165](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:165) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:273](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:273)\n- **Selection distinguishes the set from focus.** Empty mode gives an instruction before showing setters. A filled checkbox and row fill identify selected rows, and “1 of 14 selected” plus “Mark 1 done” make the target count concrete. The visible checkbox is roughly 20px inside a 48px target, and tapping the row also toggles it. [android/selection-empty.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/selection-empty.png) · [android/selection-one.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/selection-one.png) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:271](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:271) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:96](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:96)\n- **Receipts have a sound vocabulary.** Pending, confirmed, failed, and unknown outcomes have distinct words. Group receipts preserve per-outcome counts. Drafts applies the same plain-language discipline to “Local to this device · never sent.” [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:135](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:135) · [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:206](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:206) · [browser/drafts.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/drafts.png)\n\n## 6. Priority issues\n\n### P1 · Make a committing swipe predictable\n\n- **What.** A fast 64px gesture can commit at over 0.5px/ms, but the label first renders at 128px. The release recomputes average velocity, so a short swipe can also look armed during movement and cancel if the user pauses. Selection changes what a tap does, yet the pointer handlers still allow an explicit single-row action.\n- **Why it matters.** The visual preview can lag behind a mutation. In a mode that foregrounds a selected set, the same gesture can remove a different conversation.\n- **Fix.** In `useSwipeAction.ts`, start with a single distance rule, `Math.max(128, rowWidth * 0.35)`, and use it for both arming and release. In `InboxList.vue`, guard swipe start and commit with `!selecting.value` and cancel any pending or active swipe when selection begins. Keep release-to-cancel when the thumb moves back below the threshold and retain the reduced-motion path.\n- **Evidence.** [browser/swipe-done.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/swipe-done.png) · [browser/swipe-done-armed.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/swipe-done-armed.png) · [src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:71](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:71) · [src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:116](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:116) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:366](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:366) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:750](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:750)\n\n### P1 · Give Undo a legible end and a handoff\n\n- **What.** Undo expires ten seconds after acceptance. The captured settled notice offers no visible time cue, then disappears. Compact rendering suppresses existing expiry guidance when a clean result settles.\n- **Why it matters.** A distracted user returns to a changed inbox with no reminder of the subject or recovery route. A keyboard user focused on Undo can lose that control at expiry.\n- **Fix.** Under the action subject, show “Undo for Ns” from the authoritative deadline. Keep the ticking number out of the live announcement and announce the expiry once. Retain the last confirmed Mark done receipt for another 8 seconds, or until the next action, with “Moved to Done” and a 48px “Open Done” button using the existing destination. For a bulk action, offer “View in Activity” and say recovery is per conversation. If Undo held focus, transfer focus to the replacement control. Keep the backend deadline authoritative.\n- **Evidence.** [android/inbox-undo-toast.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox-undo-toast.png) · [android/inbox-after-undo-window.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox-after-undo-window.png) · [crates/mail-core/src/clock.rs:56](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/crates/mail-core/src/clock.rs:56) · [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:261](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:261) · [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:317](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:317)\n\n### P1 · Keep bulk targeting and read state legible\n\n- **What.** The checkbox replaces the read marker using `display: none`, removing its accessible state label with it. “Select N loaded” is terse but its touch-visible wording omits which mail is excluded. The largest batch takes two adjacent actions, Select all and Mark done, with no intervening review step.\n- **Why it matters.** This mode is exactly where users need to distinguish read, unread and unknown and verify an unusually large change. Weight conveys some of that to sighted users only.\n- **Fix.** Keep a distinct read-state cue by the sender in selection mode and preserve its accessible name. Give selected-row paperclips `var(--text-secondary)`. Use “Select all N shown” with a visible “Only conversations loaded in this list” line where more pages exist. For multi-row Mark done, make the bottom bar open an inline review state with “Move N conversations to Done?” and 48px “Keep selecting” and “Mark N done” controls. Keep the one-row path direct.\n- **Evidence.** [android/inbox.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox.png) · [android/selection-one.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/selection-one.png) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:302](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:302) · [src/assets/style.css:1581](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/style.css:1581) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:783](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:783) · [src/components/SelectionBar.vue:55](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/SelectionBar.vue:55) · [src/App.vue:217](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/App.vue:217)\n\n### P2 · Give the list a real bottom edge\n\n- **What.** The floating split and navigation pills cut through rows at rest. The browser scroll-end capture proves that the ordinary last row does clear them. Selection plus a notice is taller, but both are absolutely positioned and the static clearance token accounts for the bottom bar and context pill only. The list’s resize observer relies on its actual height changing. Separately, a 48px split button sits inside the 44px overflow slot.\n- **Why it matters.** Users scroll to reclaim text and checkboxes behind the controls. More text or wrapped actions can make the overlap worse. The split control also misses the intended 48px visible hit area vertically.\n- **Fix.** Give compact lists a shared in-flow footer containing the notice, selection or context, and navigation, with `.central` filling and scrolling in the remaining space. Use 8px between footer sections and let selection and notice content wrap within the footer. Put “Inbox” in the header, and omit the standalone context pill when the Inbox is current and has no alternate split. Where the split pill offers a switch, give its overflow slot the full 48px and label the primary bottom buttons “Inbox” and “Search.”\n- **Evidence.** [android/inbox.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox.png) · [browser/inbox-scrolled-end.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/inbox-scrolled-end.png) · [browser/selection-with-notice.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/selection-with-notice.png) · [src/assets/mobile/shell.css:120](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/shell.css:120) · [src/assets/mobile/shell.css:192](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/shell.css:192) · [src/assets/mobile/tokens.css:20](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/tokens.css:20) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:14](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:14) · [src/components/InboxList.vue:529](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:529)\n\n### P2 · Make the reminder sheet usable from the thumb\n\n- **What.** Three preset rows repeat the year, zone and offset, producing three lines per date in the capture. The dates also appear out of chronological order. Typing a custom time disables Schedule until the input’s separate Enter/submit path validates it. The compact rules hide the Enter hint.\n- **Why it matters.** A frequent task becomes a long form. A user who types a valid time and taps the visible primary action can encounter a disabled button with no clear next step.\n- **Fix.** Put the conversation identity and preset choices immediately under the heading. Sort dated presets by due time with Someday at the end. In compact preset rows show “Wed, Nov 18 · 8:00 AM” at 13px, state the device time zone once, and show the full instant and offset in the selected-time summary. Add a 48px “Use this time” control for edited input that calls the same validation as Enter, reports errors inline, and preserves the separate Schedule step and explicit return condition.\n- **Evidence.** [browser/reminder-picker.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/reminder-picker.png) · [src/lib/reminder-time.ts:26](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/lib/reminder-time.ts:26) · [src/lib/reminder-timezone.ts:12](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/lib/reminder-timezone.ts:12) · [src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:54](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:54) · [src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:151](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ReminderPicker.vue:151) · [src/components/RemindersList.vue:193](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/RemindersList.vue:193) · [src/assets/mobile/pages.css:229](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/pages.css:229)\n\n## 7. Persona red flags\n\n- **Alex, impatient power user.** A fast flick can remove mail before the verb registers. In the palette, “Mark done” can act on the focused conversation without naming it in the captured command list. The dispatch scope is explicit for selections, and should be just as visible for a single target. [browser/palette.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/palette.png) · [src/App.vue:1426](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/App.vue:1426)\n- **Casey, one-handed and distracted.** Menu and Select entry sit at the top. The drawer starts high and left, even though the bottom navigation is near the thumb. Returning to the screen after ten seconds turns recovery into a search for the missing conversation. [android/inbox.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox.png) · [browser/drawer.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/drawer.png) · [android/inbox-after-undo-window.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/android/inbox-after-undo-window.png)\n- **Sam, screen reader or low vision.** Native checkboxes, named navigation and a polite receipt region help. Selection removes the named read-state cue, and the attachment glyph’s muted token on the selected fill is about 2.75:1 before the unknown variant’s opacity. The disappearance of Undo and the visually clipped, keyboard-focusable checkbox outside selection deserve a TalkBack and hardware-keyboard pass. [src/components/InboxList.vue:772](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/InboxList.vue:772) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:257](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:257) · [src/assets/style.css:1581](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/style.css:1581) · [src/components/ActionNotice.vue:308](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionNotice.vue:308)\n\n## 8. Minor observations and questions\n\n- Group drawer entries by purpose with stable boundaries, for example Inbox/Starred/Done, Drafts/Reminders, and utilities. Add an in-panel Close control. The scrim, Back/Escape and focus return already provide useful foundations. The CSS declares a 180ms transform transition, while the drawer mounts at `translateX(0)`; the stills cannot establish a slide-in. [browser/drawer.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/drawer.png) · [src/components/mobile/MobileDrawer.vue:50](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/mobile/MobileDrawer.vue:50) · [src/assets/mobile/drawer.css:16](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/drawer.css:16)\n- In the palette, show the command target above the list and limit the initial touch shortlist to four relevant actions. Keep search across the full registry. Give touch users a visible Close control alongside outside tap and Escape. [browser/palette.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/palette.png) · [src/components/ActionOverlay.vue:48](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionOverlay.vue:48) · [src/components/ActionOverlay.vue:89](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/ActionOverlay.vue:89)\n- Put “No local drafts” ahead of the action in the empty state. Keep the labelled New draft button as the main action there. The top pencil currently duplicates it. [browser/drafts.png](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-review/critique-r3/browser/drafts.png) · [src/components/DraftsList.vue:295](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/DraftsList.vue:295) · [src/components/mobile/MobileTopBar.vue:55](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/components/mobile/MobileTopBar.vue:55)\n- The row sends a clear directional motion signal at 160ms and explicitly bypasses the exit animation under reduced motion. Keep the slide a cue for gesture acceptance and let the receipt communicate the provider outcome. [src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:123](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/composables/useSwipeAction.ts:123) · [src/assets/mobile/list.css:453](/home/exedev/email-os-mobile-validate/src/assets/mobile/list.css:453)\n\n1. If “Done” is where mail goes, why does the most important receipt teach “Confirmed” and leave the destination implicit?\n2. If the first four commands are the product’s opinion about how to triage, should “Compose new local draft” lead when I opened Commands from Inbox?\n3. Could someone who never learns a swipe still finish a one-handed triage session entirely from the list?\n","numLines":104,"startLine":1,"totalLines":104}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c6a60ffa-1a26-4e35-b654-c3172d677a3e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"t3-code","cwd":"/home/exedev/email-os","sessionId":"415cd333-37f5-4cfe-be88-ad6fe867d94b","version":"2.1.326","gitBranch":"main","slug":"cached-bubbling-meadow"}
